{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism","id":1524237,"title":"Intimidating werewolves into (superficially) converting to Setism","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-10-18T13:45:10-07:00","id":1524237,"title":"Intimidating werewolves into (superficially) converting to Setism","content":"Hello everyone,<br /> <br /> I'm trying to figure out how a certain thing could play out.<br /> My character in VDA20 (7th gen Warrior of Glycon) has Potence 5, Serpentis 4, Obfuscate 2, Intimidate 3, Road of Set 10, Willpower 10, all virtues maxed out. The starting concept was basically a setite saint. He's currently doing a suicide mission on a hill inhabited by Shadow Lords werewolves. The ideal plan is to kill them all stealthily, one by one. Please avoid comments on how unlikely that is: I am aware and have another character ready if this one dies. My character's first encounter with a werewolf ended with my killing him in one turn despite his being crinos (did a hold with a snake tail and Potence, he botched the roll to break free and I bled him dry), so from his point of view, as a trained stealth warrior, Set fanatic, riled up against werewolves due to some other things that have happened in the chronicle, going in the werewolves' turf alone to stealthily kill them all is not too bad of an idea. He has to kill some anyway necessarily to bring the heads to Vladimir Rustovich in exchange for some important items for the Followers of Set. Having Road of Set 10, the cause is worth the risks and he has not seen all that werewolves can do to fear them enough.<br /> <br /> My question here is about one thing in particular. The character has currently Strength 9 (and Dexterity and Stamina also 9) for the scene (reached through blood expenditure + Typhonic Avatar, hybrid form) plus Potence 5 and Intimidate 3. His snake tail has Strength 12. He has -3 to difficulty for Intimidate attempts thanks to Serpentis 1 + Serpentis 3, and another -3 if the intimidation is connected to (even forcibly) converting others to Set or glorifying him thank to his Road of Set 10 + the merit Powerful Aura. Total: -6. Intimidation attempts in VDA20 are difficulty 6, contrasted roll.<br /> One of my ideas to save my skin if the werewolves (there's plenty of them, 20 at the very least) find me is to try intimidating them into surrendering to Set or being exterminated before they go into frenzy. It's possible that the ST apply the modifier for multiple oppnents (max +4 difficulty) to my roll since I'm intimidating a lot at once. This would make my roll of Strength + Intimidate be difficulty 4 (6 is the base, +4 for multiple opponents, -6 for my bonuses). If I slam my tail to show its strength as part of the intimidation attempt and spend 1 blood point to activate Potence, I would have 5 automatic successes, plus roll 15 dice at difficulty 4 (no specialisation) for an average (mathematically) of 9 successes. Plus Potence, 14 successes. Excluding gifts or rites, the worst the Garou could roll is Strength 9 (crinos with Strength 5 + 4) + Intimidate 5 and an applicable specialisation at difficulty 6, for an average of 7 successes. I would still win by 7 successes, which is A LOT:<br /> <br /> I am aware that any Garou in frenzy is immune to being intimidated, so that's not the scenario about which I am asking. For now the werewolves are all in homid or glabro form. They've gathered for some ritual and I have stealthily killed the first of them with a tongue slice from the back. The session closed with me not having busted my Obfuscate, but there is apparently some other power going on because the ST is asking me some strange rolls to stay hidden from time to time. But they have not seen me yet, for sure, as I walked among them for a long while before going behind the last of them and killing him instantly.<br /> <br /> So the question, finally, is: what would happen at this point? Could the fear drive the Garou so deep into the Wyrm as to make them submit - even temporarily, though 7 successes (even just 5) justify durable results - to me/Set and be instantly corrupted? Or would they just make scared sounds, maybe go in their form of Rotshreck, and / or feign submission as long as I'm around? Like, how does their mind and spirit react to this kind of stuff when they're not raging?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-18T16:32:26-07:00","id":1524247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism#post1524237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So the question, finally, is: what would happen at this point?</div> </div> </div> </div>You mean, from a W:tA perspective?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Could the fear drive the Garou so deep into the Wyrm as to make them submit - even temporarily, though 7 successes (even just 5) justify durable results - to me/Set and be instantly corrupted? </div> </div> </div> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lhckuhUxcgA\" data-code=\"lhckuhUxcgA\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/lhckuhUxcgA/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> No, it usually takes BSDs a protracted and lengthy series of torture sessions for anything even remotely like that to happen to captive Garou, so a Setite isn't doing that while the Garou are up and walking. It's just going to make the Glory-mongers in the group jump to the top of the queue, because if you're really that powerful, that could be a potentially significant notch in the belt of any of them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or would they just make scared sounds, maybe go in their form of Rotshreck, and / or feign submission as long as I'm around? Like, how does their mind and spirit react to this kind of stuff when they're not raging? </div> </div> </div>Fox frenzies, you mean. They're rarely ever even seem to happen and there are a bunch of ways for them to get out of it, like a bunch of powers, spiritual patrons, and a merit or two that make it so that they literally can't enter them. And also, this show of brain-dead force isn't going to provoke it. As far as they're concerned, you're like a fomor, and they have claws to dice you apart. Also, the aforementioned &quot;killing him will increase my status and give me a sept position!&quot; factor, which for Shadow Lords, is triple platinum-weighted.<br /> <br /> Basically, how this is going to end is that at least one of them is going to replicate the &quot;I thought you were stronger!&quot; scene from the Invincible comics, but the remorse is replaced by disappointment. Or one of them has a flamethrower, also a possibility. If you name drop Rustovitch, your chances of survival drop to zero squared, because the Shadow Lords really hate him and will potentially capture and torture <b>you</b> to see what you know before leaving you out for the sun (or the flamethrower).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude00","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-18T16:47:28-07:00","id":1524249,"title":"","content":"Every ST will handle things like this differently. Some will just use dice rolls. Others will adjust the dice rolls results to something they think is more realstic. Others will want a more narrative approach.<br /> <br /> If I were the ST, I would definitely not have the Garou be so intimidated they convert to Setism.<br /> <br /> Here are the points I would be thinking of in order to determine the scene.<br /> <br /> First, this is similar to just any kind of &quot;convert or die&quot; scenario. And while in general you will always have people willing to convert (at least formally, even if not really), others will always choose death. But in such cases, it is almost always a case where the people being threatened are ALREADY defeated, disarmed, etc. and are no longer capable of defending themselves. They are at the mercy of their foe. The situation above is not that. There are various things they can do to fight or escape, all of which would likely be tried. <br /> <br /> Second, I don't care how scary or intimidating your character is, they are not going to succumb to it until you've defeated them. Especially as they are part of a group, and each Garou will likely not want to disappoint their buddies. It's a possible a single Garou on their own might choose to surrender to spare their life at a potential threat, but it's a lot less likely when part of a group. Especially if this is their pack and you have just killed one of their buddies.<br /> <br /> Third, Garou typically are already heavily indoctrinated in their own religion. Even more so in the Dark Ages setting than the modern day. They hate and detest everything that Setism is. Not only would the Garou be committing blasphemy, they would be committing treason. How many people, if threatened with death if they did not commit treason and betray their country, would actually betray their country? Surrendered soldiers have sometimes agreed to do this, but usually only in unusual circumstances (like colonial troops who fought for their colonial power, and could be shown how fighting for the other side might lead to their country's independence) and after a prolonged period of propaganda.<br /> <br /> Fourth, the Garou are not typically known for their cowardice. I have no doubt some Garou are cowards, but Garou culture as a whole stresses the martial virtues and pride in their people. We have no reason to view some Shadow Lords performing a Garou ritual are particularly cowardly. Regardless of how well a PC might roll, I wouldn't have the Garou react in that way. Fear might cause them to flee - possibly as a result of Fox Frenzy. But it wouldn't make people do a complete turn around of their values. Then again, such fear might also result in them immediately entering frenzy as their &quot;flight or fight&quot; response kicks in.<br /> <br /> Fifth, even assuming that in some bizarre situation a Garou end up surrendering, the idea that they'll be so terrified they'll immediately become corrupted just does not make sense to me. If your life was threatened, would you immediately &quot;become corrupted&quot; (whatever that means) and drop everything you believed in? No. You might act very cowardly and dishonorably to save your life in the immediate circumstances, but there would be nothing behind it. No sincerity. &quot;Corruption&quot; isn't some magical effect. It's someone choosing to become morally impure. You are not tempting the person in anyway to succumb to their base desires or animal pleasures. You would just be scaring them.<br /> <br /> Sixth, as powerful as your PC might be, Garou often fight spirits and monsters that are even more powerful. Vampires can be powerful threats, but to Garou they just don't compare to say a Nexus Crawler or Thunderwyrms. Not all Garou are capable of fighting off such evils by themselves, but they are aware of them.<br /> <br /> Most likely if you present yourself as a terrible threat, their flight or fight response kicks in, and they either succumb to frenzy and attack you, or keep their wits and attack you. That is how I would run it as an ST.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-18T18:45:33-07:00","id":1524250,"title":"","content":"I will mention that when werewolves conduct their religious rituals, it tends to be in a place of spiritual power, one that they've secured with mystical wards and other forms of protection/defense. And having a vampire show up there, even if they're not there actively murdering people, will likely send them into a violent rage. <br /> <br /> This is also a culture with a very strong &quot;death before dishonor&quot; streak, and are likely to prefer fighting to the death over surrender or forced conversion. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-18T22:48:58-07:00","id":1524258,"title":"","content":"In the werewolf section there was/is an awesome topic about the Followers of Set and how they are perfect antagonists in a Garou story<br /> There we came to the conclusion, the main power of the Settite lies in the clever wits and words, like a tempting snake (sorry...) not any supernatural poison, cool powers or earthshattering might<br /> Don't forget, the Followers of Set's main goal was/is to tear off the Chains of Ma'at, the Order especially relegious order and conditioning pf the other gods - this could and should include Gaia too; in theor whole existence the Children of Rebellion trained themselves to plant the seeds of doubt and poke the cracks of on the wall of &quot;faith&quot;, whisper in to the ear, show the truth and slowly turn someone away and convert to their God and they were quite good in it!<br /> The Garou in my humble opinion are not exeptions<br /> (, I mean the tricking/foolig the Garou to do something stupid; when the Ananasi achived their mission to aim the Garou to assault Malfeas and they cracked the Opel their Queen was kept in, so communication re-established with the Spider-folk and their Creator...)<br /> Corruption is a road to revelation for them<br /> They refer to themselves as the Mesu Bedshet, the &quot;Children of Rebellion&quot;.\u200b<br /> They seek to subvert and destroy whatever is good, noble, safe or beautiful within both Kindred and mortal society. They are plotters without peer, with an unequalled genius for misdirection and temptation. Their favorite weapons include drugs, sex, money, power, and vice in all its aspects. But for most of the Followers of Set, this corruption is just a means to an end. They believe in a radical split between the divine, eternal soul and the corrupt, transitory world and body. The laws of their faith say that deep down, the soul knows it does not belong in a prison of matter. Anything that distracts the soul from this knowledge and ties it to the world must be a trap \u2013 and if the world is a prison, then the gods must be its jailers. To achieve liberation, a soul must shed all the false doctrines, cravings and habits that bind it and blind it. Such self-awareness cannot come from mere argument or faith. Only direct experience of the most extreme sort can break the hold of the gods over the soul and allow liberation. Through these experiences, the Setites believe they can achieve truly divine power over the world and liberate themselves from every physical and spiritual constraint, as they already have taken the first steps out of the prison when they received the Embrace.<br /> The Setites corrupt you socially and mentally, while the Baali can corrupt you in supernatural ways. The Setites try to make you change your mind, the Baali try to mess with your soul. You can also see that in the way, the two lines convert people. Setites have some members in their community, that are actually from other clans. Physically they remain members of those clans, they just follow a different religion. When Baali convert someone, this vampire gets another weakness and set of disciplines, they change the curse if you will.<br /> It is true, though, that the ways these two act are very similar and that the too in the past often clashed with each other, because they occupy the same niche, so to say. But while both try to destroy the status quo, Setites do that for liberation (even if it often enough ends up in the exact opposite) and Baali do that to either shatter the universe or, in some certain cases, to prevent that from happening by feeding great old ones with a little bit of destruction to keep them pleased.<br /> The BSD is basically the &quot;Baali of the Garou Nation&quot;; actually th Followers of Set and the Black Spiral Dancers have very little in common, even potentially enemies.<br /> A bravem bold or fool settite maybe would try to use the BSD as pawns... dangerous game, like working with a Bane Mummy (a Bane Mummy is more likely to work with the Black Spiral Dancers than the Followers of Set in my humble oppinion)<br /> Of course there are corrupt Settites who &quot;failed&quot; their own quest to free himself and others from the web of Ma'at, the Order that Shackles and basically a failed acolyte, or an Apep worshipper, more or less heretic who belives Set serves Apep and he is its champion....<br /> Similarly, there are Black Spiral Dancers who are sane/insane enough to belive, their oal is to kill Gaiai, yes, but to save the world, because a killed spirit will reborn, and to save Gaia they have to kill her to hasten her rebirth, a hard reboot of Earth<br /> Actually a Settite have more in common with a common Garou who belives the source of all problem is the Weaver... Ma'at and Weaver are basically the same, so both parties could be allies, enemies, rivals, or dance on the blade's edge altogether<br /> Beware a Lector Pirest of the Mesu Bedshet, the &quot;Children of Rebellion&quot;!<br /> If a Settite done her homework, could be deadly foe, whispering from the shadow, avoiding your claws and fangs and slowly showing the Truth<br /> Let's say, like ol' Palpatine to Anakin...<br /> One by one... eventually even a whole Tribe could fell to serve the God of the Storms<br /> No, not Grandfather Thunder, Sutekh! ^^\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude00","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T04:54:46-07:00","id":1524265,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23890_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23890\" data-vbnamecard=\"23890\">Shadeprowler</a> That's all nice and well. But that's not intimidating a group of Garou into following Set in one Intimidate roll on their Caern grounds.<br /> <br /> Also, I do take objection to identifying Ma'at and the Weaver as the same thing. They are decidedly not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T05:02:34-07:00","id":1524266,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524247#post1524247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You mean, from a W:tA perspective?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, for W:tA because I know it only superficially.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524247#post1524247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, it usually takes BSDs a protracted and lengthy series of torture sessions for anything even remotely like that to happen to captive Garou, so a Setite isn't doing that while the Garou are up and walking.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Reason why I asked is that I don't know how Garou are corrupted by the Wyrm, but I got a sense there was some concrete change linked to it, so I wondered if a strong and significant event in which the Garou betray their ideals out of fear would have some concrete corrupting consequence, like Gaia turning away and the Wyrm changing them and making them its own. Not because Set and the Wyrm are necessarily linked, but because Garou fear and the Wyrm are. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524247#post1524247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's just going to make the Glory-mongers in the group jump to the top of the queue, because if you're really that powerful, that could be a potentially significant notch in the belt of any of them.<br /> <br /> Fox frenzies, you mean. They're rarely ever even seem to happen and there are a bunch of ways for them to get out of it, like a bunch of powers, spiritual patrons, and a merit or two that make it so that they literally can't enter them. And also, this show of brain-dead force isn't going to provoke it. As far as they're concerned, you're like a fomor, and they have claws to dice you apart. Also, the aforementioned &quot;killing him will increase my status and give me a sept position!&quot; factor, which for Shadow Lords, is triple platinum-weighted.<br /> <br /> Basically, how this is going to end is that at least one of them is going to replicate the &quot;I thought you were stronger!&quot; scene from the Invincible comics, but the remorse is replaced by disappointment. Or one of them has a flamethrower, also a possibility. If you name drop Rustovitch, your chances of survival drop to zero squared, because the Shadow Lords really hate him and will potentially capture and torture <b>you</b> to see what you know before leaving you out for the sun (or the flamethrower).</div> </div> </div> </div>I understand the rationale of that assessment and respect it if a ST decides to play it out that way. Personally I think that both the needs of narrative in a RPG and the game system make it so that acts of intimidation are intended to be depowering. Or in other words: if nobody used intimidation, and the vampire just appeared really strong while fighting, the reactions you mentioned would make sense to me, and Garou would likely do things that would lead to Rage rolls and entering rage too during the fight, or they might rage as soon as they discover their sept-mates have been killed by an agent of the Wyrm invading their caern; if however an action to intimidate is declared before the Garou Rage, I would not, as a ST, ever rule fight-or-flight as reaction to intimidation - whether mundane or supernatural. If an intimidation attempt is successul after having factored in the roll the intended victim's Strength (I used the highest a Garou can have without some gift, which is 9 in Crinos), her Intimidation dots (I used the highest a Garou can have, which is 5), and her number advantage (I appl\u0323ied to my own roll +4 difficulty which is the most you can have with multiple opponents), unless other gifts are used, I would narratively conclude that the intimidating character is indeed SO able to summon his victims' fears that he blocks them in their tracks and makes them act cowed - as the action &quot;Intimidate&quot; states in VDA20, and they would therefore not attack at the very least. 7 successes, which could even match or exceed some of the Garous' permanent or current Willpower rating, would indeed indicate something on the level of a religious experience almost, something that could make them change perspective on life. 3 successes are enough usually to get what one wants - in this case that they at least in the moment bend the knee and refrain from attaccking - and 5 success give great or durable results. These are 7 successes.<br /> <br /> I understand Garou are hard to scare: in fact they're immune to fear while frenzying and have other rites and gifts that very likely help them. I understand a ST may therefore ignore successful intimidation rolls. Personally I think it makes more sense given the game system to not ignore exceptional successes attained even after having factored in Garou advantages. No matter how strong werewolves are, some things that go bump in the night are sometimes either stronger or better able to mess with their heads, and I think that's narratively cool. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T05:16:37-07:00","id":1524267,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524250#post1524250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I will mention that when werewolves conduct their religious rituals, it tends to be in a place of spiritual power, one that they've secured with mystical wards and other forms of protection/defense. And having a vampire show up there, even if they're not there actively murdering people, will likely send them into a violent rage.<br /> <br /> This is also a culture with a very strong &quot;death before dishonor&quot; streak, and are likely to prefer fighting to the death over surrender or forced conversion.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, we're actually around their caern, although the ritual they're doing now is not centered on the caern. They knew I was prowling around the area but could not find me earlier as I left it for an hour and then came back, always obfuscated and with 13 dice and advantages even on mundane sneaking rolls. They set up a trap - a building full of tar with a Garou who could ignite his skin inside and some kind of spirit clapping hands who would clap near where I was - but I foiled it burning the building from the outside without giving away my position and the clapping spirits have stopped tracking me.<br /> Current theory, mentioned by the ST as a possibility, is that the house burning was a sign for them that I should be dead. The ST may be playing with our heads, so it's currently unknown whether they know I am still around, have moved and have started killing. From their behaviour, they don't seem to know and might indeed assume me dead.<br /> <br /> Of course a caern can have all sorts of protections. My character does not know, though, so he dared it anyway, and it's the ST who has chosen what to give the sept and what not to give them. I have had to make odd rolls to stay hidden when even obfuscate would not have demanded it, so they surely have something up to detect me, but I've foiled it so far, apparently. But during my first exploration of the perimeter of the area, a child spotted either me, or that the Wyrm was there, warned the sept and I retreated for an hour to see what would happen. Obfuscate can be penetrated by children, but I am also sneaking, so even where obfuscate fails, mundane detecting me is unlikely to work, even with Garou senses - excluding some gifts. I have the merit &quot;Leave no trace&quot; too, so no scent or footprint tracking against me. I think the kid just used &quot;Sense the Wyrm&quot; and felt my presence and general direction.<br /> <br /> Anyway, in brief: what they have up their sleeves is up to the ST. So far, I'm alive and apparently undetected.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T06:02:25-07:00","id":1524271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524265#post1524265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's all nice and well. But that's not intimidating a group of Garou into following Set in one Intimidate roll on their Caern grounds.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed<br /> A smart settite tempt and seduce, convert and show you the &quot;truth&quot;, not force faith, since that is impossible <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524265#post1524265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Also, I do take objection to identifying Ma'at and the Weaver as the same thing. They are decidedly not.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would say it is debatable<br /> Ma\u02bdat comprised the ancient <i>Egyptian</i> concepts of truth, balance, order, harmony, law, morality, and justice\u200b<br /> She is the fundamental order of the universe and the personification of cosmic order<br /> (The) \u200bIsfet (meaning &quot;injustice&quot;, &quot;chaos&quot;, or &quot;violence&quot;; as a verb, \u201cto do evil\u201d) was the counter to Ma'at, which was order. Isfet did not have a physical form. Rather, it was believed that the Isfet was personified in the form of Apep.<br /> The Amenti and their predecessors, the Shemsu-Heru, adhere to the strict tenures of the Ma'at in order to be seen as worthy before the Judges of Ma'at to be blessed with resurrection. Certain actions (such as lying, murdering, and addictive behaviors) are violations of the universal principle of justice. As agents of Ma'at, mummies are expected to avoid such transgressions.<br /> <br /> Now what is the Weaver?<br /> Order and Stasis<br /> The Mummies represent everything what Weaver is actually, followers of Ma'at and Osiris, rigid Faith and order and more importantly, they never change... not even die<br /> They are one of the most unnatural things you can find, something that escaped basically one of the fundamental forces of the Universe, Death itself... they are ever living abominations<br /> <br /> The Followers of Set also use the term Ma'at to refer to the chains the Aeons placed upon all living souls to keep them subservient to them and entangled in the illusions of the material world. Ma'at is seen as a force of ultimate stasis that strives to imprison mankind into the base world of matter, denying them their true potential. The Setites break these chains through the Gates of Revelation, through rage, lust, ambition and desire that eventually lead to freedom and ascendence (according to Clanbook: Setites Revised,)<br /> <br /> Se even if they are not the same, there are more than enough similarites (or actually they could be the same or even faces of each other or aspects) for a Settite to is that as a ground work in the temptation process or to convince a Garou, they are on the same side, and politely remind the furball, Sutekh stood on the Million Yerar's Old Ship of Ra, the Sun and fought against Apep valiantly (and most of the Setite are not Apep worhsipers, quite the opposite) and could have a common enemy... and lend help in the form of influence, money, weapons... servants.... dirty secrets or whatever the Garou little heart desire to further the will of Gaia, Amen!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T06:05:55-07:00","id":1524272,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524249#post1524249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Every ST will handle things like this differently. Some will just use dice rolls. Others will adjust the dice rolls results to something they think is more realstic. Others will want a more narrative approach.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed, it's down to each ST. I like to factor all considerations in, determine difficulties and penalties or bonuses accordingly, and then let the roll decide, even if it's an amazing roll that indicates something unheard of. That's my thing, but I accept other approaches. The rest of my answer is just to chat about different perspectives and why I see it differently. I already think your answer makes sense and I am not criticising it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524249#post1524249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">First, this is similar to just any kind of &quot;convert or die&quot; scenario. And while in general you will always have people willing to convert (at least formally, even if not really), others will always choose death. But in such cases, it is almost always a case where the people being threatened are ALREADY defeated, disarmed, etc. and are no longer capable of defending themselves. They are at the mercy of their foe. The situation above is not that. There are various things they can do to fight or escape, all of which would likely be tried.</div> </div> </div> </div>Obviously I meant a superficial bending of the knee to save one's life, though with 7 successes, as a ST, I would rule some kind of life changing religious experience - especially seen as Serpentis and the bearing of a Road rating of 10 with Powerful Aura are involved, so my character is actually a sort of saint and meetings with him could be life changing. The issue of trying other things first, or some characters preferring death, etc. I think is all part of the factors of the intimidation roll. To me what you described, of being defeated first, choosing death, etc. is simply how coercion would go without using intimidate. You kill or incapacitate those who don't surrender and it ends there. Intimidation is what allows you to get people to do what you want before you actually need to beat them to a pulp. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524249#post1524249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Second, I don't care how scary or intimidating your character is, they are not going to succumb to it until you've defeated them. Especially as they are part of a group, and each Garou will likely not want to disappoint their buddies. It's a possible a single Garou on their own might choose to surrender to spare their life at a potential threat, but it's a lot less likely when part of a group. Especially if this is their pack and you have just killed one of their buddies.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed, but that's why there's a +4 penalty to the intimidation roll. Normally this would mean the roll itself, which has a base difficulty of 6, goes to 10, while their roll (it's contrasted) stays at 6, so even a character with many dice more than the strongest Garou would still fail, rolling less successes. Statistically, without specialisation (which in VDA20 gives 2 successes for each 10 rolled), rolls at difficulty 10 score 0 successes regardless of the dice pool because each 10 can be cancelled by a 1. However my character has supernatural bonuses to intimidation reducing the difficulty by -6 here. That is completely unusual and justifiably counters how normally Garou would act against a powerful foe, unless they use frenzy or gifts to resist intimidation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524249#post1524249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Third, Garou typically are already heavily indoctrinated in their own religion. Even more so in the Dark Ages setting than the modern day. They hate and detest everything that Setism is. Not only would the Garou be committing blasphemy, they would be committing treason. How many people, if threatened with death if they did not commit treason and betray their country, would actually betray their country? Surrendered soldiers have sometimes agreed to do this, but usually only in unusual circumstances (like colonial troops who fought for their colonial power, and could be shown how fighting for the other side might lead to their country's independence) and after a prolonged period of propaganda.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed, but this is included in the system. Rolls to interrogate and torture, for example, are done against the victim's Willpower, and the victim's nature can also affect the roll to raise difficulties. So it's the same issue as above: I am asking them to surrender at least nominally out of fear, I am counting all their advantages as they are quantified in the system, I add my advantages in, do the maths and come out (against the most optimised Garou to resist me, with Strength 9, Intimidation 5 and specialised in this stuff) with 7 successes, when the game system says you get what you want with 3 successes and have some great or durable result with 5. It seems actually reasonable to me, narratively, to conclude that the advantages my character has from Serpentis and the Road bearing are so effective that they would lead the Garou to at least doubt their beliefs and wonder whether Set could be more powerful than both the Wyrm and Gaia and whether he's not more deserving of allegiance, if a mere servant of his - probably first they encounter in Eastern Europe - has such a grasp on their minds and has been wondering unseen in their caern and managed to foil their traps and kill some of them. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524249#post1524249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then again, such fear might also result in them immediately entering frenzy as their &quot;flight or fight&quot; response kicks in.</div> </div> </div> </div>I might have said in another answer to another member above, but I don't think &quot;fight-or-flight&quot; should be applied in this way to the game system. &quot;Fight-or-flight&quot; is a response to perceived stress or threat. In the game, both Garou and vampires divide threats/stressful occurrences between those that cause frenzy/rage and those that cause rotschreck/fox fear. Allowing character, even humans, to react to natural or supernatural provocation with flight instead of fight, and to natural or supernatural intimidation with fight instead of flight, would totally break the system. Game-wise, characters fight intimidation back if they win the contrasted rolls for it and decide to fight as a reaction, or if they take the failed attempt as an insult and go into frenzy. If the attempt succeeds, and the victim is not immune due to being already in frenzy, the victim is cowed and surely does not attack. It may even not run away, if the intimidation was &quot;flee and I'll kill you&quot; and enough successes were rolled. It may just freeze, or do as they're told until the threat has gone. And with multiple successes, it would be like mafia or abusive partners scaring their victims so much that they fear taking action even when the intimidating individual is gone, out of sheer fear of the consequences should they hear about what the victim did against them (ex.: going to the police). <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524249#post1524249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fifth, even assuming that in some bizarre situation a Garou end up surrendering, the idea that they'll be so terrified they'll immediately become corrupted just does not make sense to me. If your life was threatened, would you immediately &quot;become corrupted&quot; (whatever that means) and drop everything you believed in? No. You might act very cowardly and dishonorably to save your life in the immediate circumstances, but there would be nothing behind it. No sincerity. &quot;Corruption&quot; isn't some magical effect. It's someone choosing to become morally impure. You are not tempting the person in anyway to succumb to their base desires or animal pleasures. You would just be scaring them.</div> </div> </div> </div>What I meant with corruption was some supernatural change as Gaia abandons them and the Wyrm claims them as his for strongly bertraying Garou ideals. I don't know W:tA system well, but I know there are some cases where the Wyrm actually changes Garou or takes partial control of them. Hence my question. Otherwise, yes: normal humans don't get &quot;corrupted&quot; by fear. At most, they get traumatised. Although, when it comes to religious conviction (or even the rules of some Roads different from Humanity), sometimes giving in to fear could call into question one's faith and lead the individual to turn away from his/her belief either because they did not protect them, or because they despair of redemption after such apostacy. And finally, one of the Revelations of Setite mysteries is the Revelation of Fear. Safety is a base desire. Showing people that they are slaves to fear, and that none is worth that fear but Set, is a totally sensible conversion step for a Setite, especially the warrior ilk.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524249#post1524249\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sixth, as powerful as your PC might be, Garou often fight spirits and monsters that are even more powerful. Vampires can be powerful threats, but to Garou they just don't compare to say a Nexus Crawler or Thunderwyrms. Not all Garou are capable of fighting off such evils by themselves, but they are aware of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure this applies. It would be like saying that vampires should not be scared of Garou because they know Methuselahs and Antediluvians can slaughter them by the pack. I am surely not as strong as a Nexus Crawler, and I don't even know the rules for them, but perhaps most Garou would be terrorized by them without gifts to resist the fear they inspire, or perhaps Nexus Crawlers don't bother with intimidating actions because they rather kill their enemies straight away, or perhaps my vampire, with -6 to intimidating difficulty, is way weaker than a Nexus Crawler, but somehow inspires way more irrational fear. Who knows? Probably this sept has never met someone like me. Maybe they haven't even determined that I am a vampire yet.<br /> <br /> Sorry if any part of the reply seemed disrespectful. Not my intention, and I thank you because I enjoyed reflecting on the game system and answering.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T07:33:22-07:00","id":1524276,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524272#post1524272\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Agreed, it's down to each ST. I like to factor all considerations in, determine difficulties and penalties or bonuses accordingly, and then let the roll decide, even if it's an amazing roll that indicates something unheard of. That's my thing, but I accept other approaches. The rest of my answer is just to chat about different perspectives and why I see it differently. I already think your answer makes sense and I am not criticising it.<br /> <br /> Obviously I meant a superficial bending of the knee to save one's life, though with 7 successes, as a ST, I would rule some kind of life changing religious experience - especially seen as Serpentis and the bearing of a Road rating of 10 with Powerful Aura are involved, so my character is actually a sort of saint and meetings with him could be life changing. The issue of trying other things first, or some characters preferring death, etc. I think is all part of the factors of the intimidation roll. To me what you described, of being defeated first, choosing death, etc. is simply how coercion would go without using intimidate. You kill or incapacitate those who don't surrender and it ends there. Intimidation is what allows you to get people to do what you want before you actually need to beat them to a pulp.</div> </div> </div> </div>Strength + Intimidation is a bogus roll. You seriously shouldn't have gotten anywhere near that number of successes, because just walking up and whipping your vampire dick out is going to be seen as a challenge and not an action that makes them pause for thought. You have used a very bad approach to try and accomplish your ends, and should probably have just died then and there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Agreed, but that's why there's a +4 penalty to the intimidation roll. Normally this would mean the roll itself, which has a base difficulty of 6, goes to 10, while their roll (it's contrasted) stays at 6, so even a character with many dice more than the strongest Garou would still fail, rolling less successes. Statistically, without specialisation (which in VDA20 gives 2 successes for each 10 rolled), rolls at difficulty 10 score 0 successes regardless of the dice pool because each 10 can be cancelled by a 1. However my character has supernatural bonuses to intimidation reducing the difficulty by -6 here. That is completely unusual and justifiably counters how normally Garou would act against a powerful foe, unless they use frenzy or gifts to resist intimidation. </div> </div> </div>Don't the 20th anniversary editions cap difficulty at 9?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Agreed, but this is included in the system. Rolls to interrogate and torture, for example, are done against the victim's Willpower, and the victim's nature can also affect the roll to raise difficulties. So it's the same issue as above: I am asking them to surrender at least nominally out of fear, I am counting all their advantages as they are quantified in the system, I add my advantages in, do the maths and come out (against the most optimised Garou to resist me, with Strength 9, Intimidation 5 and specialised in this stuff) with 7 successes, when the game system says you get what you want with 3 successes and have some great or durable result with 5. It seems actually reasonable to me, narratively, to conclude that the advantages my character has from Serpentis and the Road bearing are so effective that they would lead the Garou to at least doubt their beliefs and wonder whether Set could be more powerful than both the Wyrm and Gaia and whether he's not more deserving of allegiance, if a mere servant of his - probably first they encounter in Eastern Europe - has such a grasp on their minds and has been wondering unseen in their caern and managed to foil their traps and kill some of them. </div> </div> </div>No, at best they might make the Garou regroup. Making someone rethink their entire existence would probably be an extended test requiring 50-100 successes.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I might have said in another answer to another member above, but I don't think &quot;fight-or-flight&quot; should be applied in this way to the game system. &quot;Fight-or-flight&quot; is a response to perceived stress or threat. In the game, both Garou and vampires divide threats/stressful occurrences between those that cause frenzy/rage and those that cause rotschreck/fox fear. Allowing character, even humans, to react to natural or supernatural provocation with flight instead of fight, and to natural or supernatural intimidation with fight instead of flight, would totally break the system. Game-wise, characters fight intimidation back if they win the contrasted rolls for it and decide to fight as a reaction, or if they take the failed attempt as an insult and go into frenzy. If the attempt succeeds, and the victim is not immune due to being already in frenzy, the victim is cowed and surely does not attack. It may even not run away, if the intimidation was &quot;flee and I'll kill you&quot; and enough successes were rolled. It may just freeze, or do as they're told until the threat has gone. And with multiple successes, it would be like mafia or abusive partners scaring their victims so much that they fear taking action even when the intimidating individual is gone, out of sheer fear of the consequences should they hear about what the victim did against them (ex.: going to the police). </div> </div> </div>Again, some effects make Garou immune to fox frenzies. Mars Midsky, an appropriate spiritual patron, or the Gift Wearing the Bear Shirt are all ways to do this, and they aren't alone by any means. So, yes, Garou can react to attempts to frighten them by getting pissed and attacking. Their Rage far, far outstrips the vampiric Beast in its fury.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What I meant with corruption was some supernatural change as Gaia abandons them and the Wyrm claims them as his for strongly bertraying Garou ideals. I don't know W:tA system well, but I know there are some cases where the Wyrm actually changes Garou or takes partial control of them. Hence my question. Otherwise, yes: normal humans don't get &quot;corrupted&quot; by fear. At most, they get traumatised. Although, when it comes to religious conviction (or even the rules of some Roads different from Humanity), sometimes giving in to fear could call into question one's faith and lead the individual to turn away from his/her belief either because they did not protect them, or because they despair of redemption after such apostacy. And finally, one of the Revelations of Setite mysteries is the Revelation of Fear. Safety is a base desire. Showing people that they are slaves to fear, and that none is worth that fear but Set, is a totally sensible conversion step for a Setite, especially the warrior ilk. </div> </div> </div>That really only happens if they dance the spiral or summon and surrender to a Maeljin Incarna. Given what blunders they've perpetrated while still having the favor of Naturae and other Gaian spirits, I'm surprised that you imagined that there was any scenario where it would happen, because that's been some heavy shit and they're still with Gaia, who you can think of somewhat more as a force than a person to talk to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not sure this applies. It would be like saying that vampires should not be scared of Garou because they know Methuselahs and Antediluvians can slaughter them by the pack. I am surely not as strong as a Nexus Crawler, and I don't even know the rules for them, but perhaps most Garou would be terrorized by them without gifts to resist the fear they inspire, or perhaps Nexus Crawlers don't bother with intimidating actions because they rather kill their enemies straight away, or perhaps my vampire, with -6 to intimidating difficulty, is way weaker than a Nexus Crawler, but somehow inspires way more irrational fear. Who knows? Probably this sept has never met someone like me. Maybe they haven't even determined that I am a vampire yet.<br /> <br /> Sorry if any part of the reply seemed disrespectful. Not my intention, and I thank you because I enjoyed reflecting on the game system and answering.<br /> </div> </div> </div>Nexus Crawlers are walking unreality bubbles that can warp what is into something that it shouldn't be - terrible Lovecraftian shit that would probably make even Methusalahs enter the Red Fear. They tend to look the part, too, with an array of limbs that each sport nasty blades and hooks and several mouths, each with more teeth than the entire Osmond family (this is purely cosmetic, it still rolls its Rage of 10 to deal damage no matter what it looks like). Even if a 4th gen were to avoid running away, they'd probably soon wish that they had, since when I say it can warp reality, I'm not kidding - they give examples that range from the innocuous (do a little Vicissitude 1, or alter the local temperature a bit) to the daunting (transform steel into silver, change the floor into mud, change sweat into highly corrosive acid) and to the pants-shitting (make all the air incredibly toxic, delete someone's entire face, change bones into something rotten that's not even remotely up to the challenge of supporting muscles or the weight of the body, turn huge stretches of the ground into silver). Also, while its physical, materialized body has only 12 health levels, its spiritual substance provides somewhere between 30 and a 100 points of Essence, which act as no-penalty health levels.<br /> <br /> The mechanics for Garou are to roll Willpower to avoid a fox frenzy. I suppose that vampires would have to fall back to Courage instead. No other directions are given for how many successes or what difficulty.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T15:09:04-07:00","id":1524291,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524276#post1524276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Strength + Intimidation is a bogus roll. You seriously shouldn't have gotten anywhere near that number of successes, because just walking up and whipping your vampire dick out is going to be seen as a challenge and not an action that makes them pause for thought. You have used a very bad approach to try and accomplish your ends, and should probably have just died then and there.</div> </div> </div> </div>I genuinely don't get the reason behind any of these sentences. I don't know why Strength + Intimidation would be a bogus roll, as it's been the standard for physical intimidation in various editions. It may not be perfect for all situations, but one would need to analyse specifics to choose alternatives.<br /> I don't know why I should not have gotten near that number of successes when I have all the reasons in the system to do so: between Potence, blood and VDA20's Serpentis I have very high but perfectly within the system Strength levels; I am competent at Intimidate (3 dots); I have 3 factors that together reduce the difficulty to intimidation rolls by 3 normally and by 6 if conversion is involved. Any factor in game that reduces the difficulty of actions has to be interpreted as quite significant because it moves even the &quot;rarest, most absurd actions&quot; (VDA20, p.322), i.e. difficulty 10 actions, to being merely &quot;extremely difficult&quot; (p.323), i.e. difficulty 9, or less. It's totally fine for me to say that what I'm trying to do is in the &quot;rarest, most absurd actions&quot; range, but I have supernatural advantages to make that drop to an &quot;easy&quot; (p.323), i.e. difficulty 4, because I have - I repeat - 3 specific supernatural advantages to do just that, just like it's absurd Garou would be immune to a ton of things, but they have specific supernatural advantages to have that immunity nonetheless.<br /> Putting aside the benefits of Serpentis in VDA20 to reducing intimidation difficulties, one should think carefully about - and imagine accordingly for the game narrative - what it means for a character to have 10 dots in their Road and the merit Powerful Aura, reducing by 3 the difficulties of all interactions with humans plus all the attempts of a specific thing determined by the Road itself - in the case of the Road of Set in VDA20 it is unfortunately not written in the book due to some likely editing oversight, but at our table we agreed it's similar to how it is in VtM20: conversions. Plus religious actions and appearing as some divine manifestations instead of the true faith resistance bonus given in VtM20. I think it is perfectly legitimate by how the game is written that a vampire with such Road rating and merit, on this Road, for whom the most absurd and rarest (difficulty 10) of conversions become merely &quot;challenging&quot; (p.323, for difficulty 7 actions), may be one of those people that really turn around the lives of those who meet him. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524276#post1524276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't the 20th anniversary editions cap difficulty at 9?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, VDA20 caps it at 10.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524276#post1524276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, at best they might make the Garou regroup. Making someone rethink their entire existence would probably be an extended test requiring 50-100 successes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't quite see why. Humans rethink their existence quite often, and strong encounters play often a stronger role in this than long and slow conditioning. Radical changes are rare either way, but so is meeting a vampire with what I have. Resistance to such changes is usually represented by Willpower and Conscience / Conviction (which Garou don't have). Willpower is ranked 1 to 10, with humans rarely having more than 5, and supernaturals having usually something more, though starting vampires and Garou alike won't have more than 5 without spending freebie points. Garou have significantly shorter lifespans than vampires, so it's not a given that they will cultivate Willpower to its peak. Some will, most won't. Even most vampires won't. Garou's psychological resistance is mainly due to their Rage. Maybe it could be argued that in this scenario Gnosis might offer a bonus as it represents the lived and instinctual experience of the truth of Garou beliefs about the spirit world, but nobody is trying to convince them that the spirit world is false, so the relevance of Gnosis here is debatable.<br /> Aside from mechanics, the game plot is full of supernaturals setting up cults around themselves, sometimes with unlikely followers. Other than their higher Willpower or specific powers, supernaturals have no special resistance to the fascination of religious beliefs or religious people, and even to changing them. Most of them either follow a human faith and are touched by the saints of said faiths, or they profess some supernatural faith and are touched by the &quot;pious&quot; of that faith, and may well rethink their beliefs if these are shaken by rare encounters. A vampire with 10 in any Road is a rare encounter, even if it's a 13th gen nobody for anything else, and a vampire with 10 in a Road that mystically empowers conversion - the Serpent was, after all, the top inspirer of doubt - is even rarer. Sure, unless there is work done later to consolidate and nurture faith, even the memory of such a rare encounter might well fade like a dream, but at least in the moment I don't think it's off the table that they feel overwhelmed by a fear they never felt before and bend the knee and avoid attacking the stranger who showed up in their midst after doing things others have not even dared before. <br /> I can't think of anything in the game system of any of the WoD splats that requires 50-100 successes. Is there some spell in M:tA perhaps that does? Conditioning, the 4th power of Dominate, requires 50 successes max, and is intended as a long term total mental control. Here we are talking about a single intense experience caused by 2 powers (Serpentis 1 and 3 of VDA20) and something rarer and truer than disciplines - high Road ratings, which have a powerful social effect premised on mastery of the Beast - something even many Methuselahs can't claim to have, and nobody is asking for total Dominate-like control. In principle, I'm asking not to be attacked, for which doubt is enough, or a superficial, not heartfelt, bending of the knee, though with 7 successes, against an optimised Garou in crinos, which may even exceed the Willpower of many of those present, I would easily justify things going even further.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524276#post1524276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, some effects make Garou immune to fox frenzies. Mars Midsky, an appropriate spiritual patron, or the Gift Wearing the Bear Shirt are all ways to do this, and they aren't alone by any means. So, yes, Garou can react to attempts to frighten them by getting pissed and attacking. Their Rage far, far outstrips the vampiric Beast in its fury.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, but those are gifts and like powers. I said from the beginning they might have stuff like that, or even just frenzy, and thus be immune or more resistant than I calculated. But it's not a given that every Garou has those, so what I said about fight-or-flight stands and those special powers are just specific exceptions. Fox frenzy is not my aim anyway. Intimidation attempts, by the rules, are not meant to lead to fox frenzy or rotschreck, but to cow the victims. The goal is control of some sort, not irrational flight (let alone fight).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524276#post1524276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That really only happens if they dance the spiral or summon and surrender to a Maeljin Incarna. Given what blunders they've perpetrated while still having the favor of Naturae and other Gaian spirits, I'm surprised that you imagined that there was any scenario where it would happen, because that's been some heavy shit and they're still with Gaia, who you can think of somewhat more as a force than a person to talk to.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey, I said I don't know W:tA well and that's why I asked. No need to be surprised by what I imagined, I never claimed to know how their relationship with Gaia or the Wyrm works.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524276#post1524276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nexus Crawlers are walking unreality bubbles that can warp what is into something that it shouldn't be - terrible Lovecraftian shit that would probably make even Methusalahs enter the Red Fear. They tend to look the part, too, with an array of limbs that each sport nasty blades and hooks and several mouths, each with more teeth than the entire Osmond family (this is purely cosmetic, it still rolls its Rage of 10 to deal damage no matter what it looks like). Even if a 4th gen were to avoid running away, they'd probably soon wish that they had, since when I say it can warp reality, I'm not kidding - they give examples that range from the innocuous (do a little Vicissitude 1, or alter the local temperature a bit) to the daunting (transform steel into silver, change the floor into mud, change sweat into highly corrosive acid) and to the pants-shitting (make all the air incredibly toxic, delete someone's entire face, change bones into something rotten that's not even remotely up to the challenge of supporting muscles or the weight of the body, turn huge stretches of the ground into silver). Also, while its physical, materialized body has only 12 health levels, its spiritual substance provides somewhere between 30 and a 100 points of Essence, which act as no-penalty health levels.</div> </div> </div> </div>Cool, so they probably would have massive bonuses to terrorise people upon sight, or probably people need a Courage roll just to look at them, and the Garou that manage to go against them would have higher resistance than that by whatever means the game system offers. Still doesn't change that Garou are not immune to fear or intimidation if they're not in rage or using special powers.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T17:31:41-07:00","id":1524296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524291#post1524291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I genuinely don't get the reason behind any of these sentences. I don't know why Strength + Intimidation would be a bogus roll, as it's been the standard for physical intimidation in various editions. It may not be perfect for all situations, but one would need to analyse specifics to choose alternatives.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I pose a question to you - do you think that W:tA allows characters to use Strength + Intimidation alone to frighten others? Cut to the chase, the answer is no, it doesn't; even given the example of throwing someone across the room while in Crinos, it suggests a bonus to Manipulation + Intimidation, and you don't suffer the normal Crinos penalty to Manipulation. There are a few good reasons for this.<br /> <br /> Firstly, allowing Strength to be used in the dice pool would rather heavily flatten the curve of possible dice pools and encourage just shifting into Crinos or Hispo to make the roll. If you can change into Crinos, you always have a high dice pool at the ready, so you'd be looking at a rather homogeneous composition of competence for a pack, rather than having the Shadow Lords specialize in this kind of thing.<br /> <br /> Secondly, from a world-building perspective, Garou don't tend to be impressed by physical force alone. So you broke some stone work? Big deal! Any liquored up Garou could that that with Crinos at a construction site. If you don't have Renown, you are, at best, a nobody with potential. Just like how Status isn't just a simple matter of a Background with Garou, getting other Garou to back down is an elaborate system of mind games meant to hopefully curb the incidence of frenzied combats to the death. That said, if your Rage is too high and you're in a Facedown competition, you use that instead of Cha + Int, and rolling too many successes in one go will cause you to frenzy (and lose the contest, incidentally). Difficulty is adjusted upward for higher Rank opponents.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know why I should not have gotten near that number of successes when I have all the reasons in the system to do so: between Potence, blood and VDA20's Serpentis I have very high but perfectly within the system Strength levels; I am competent at Intimidate (3 dots); I have 3 factors that together reduce the difficulty to intimidation rolls by 3 normally and by 6 if conversion is involved. Any factor in game that reduces the difficulty of actions has to be interpreted as quite significant because it moves even the &quot;rarest, most absurd actions&quot; (VDA20, p.322), i.e. difficulty 10 actions, to being merely &quot;extremely difficult&quot; (p.323), i.e. difficulty 9, or less. It's totally fine for me to say that what I'm trying to do is in the &quot;rarest, most absurd actions&quot; range, but I have supernatural advantages to make that drop to an &quot;easy&quot; (p.323), i.e. difficulty 4, because I have - I repeat - 3 specific supernatural advantages to do just that, just like it's absurd Garou would be immune to a ton of things, but they have specific supernatural advantages to have that immunity nonetheless. </div> </div> </div>This is an extreme system assumption mismatch. Basically, the action you used, even with Potence twice as high, still wouldn't have accrued that many successes because Garou only seem to respond to mind game-style Intimidation, and higher Rank Garou, as noted, are less susceptible to being cowed. Basically, you've gotten too hung up on technical difficulties and didn't take into account that the action wasn't just rare, it was flat out impossible. You didn't make a convincing display of force, you did the Garou equivalent of &quot;You got served&quot;. An actual display of physical force that would give the Garou pause for thought is generally only accomplished by thunderwyrms or zmei, both of which are not just possessed of high Strength ratings, they're also fucking huge and capable of crushing Garou, Fera, or vampires under their weight just by moving or falling. This is the world that they live in, and if you're not prepared to bring that to the table, maybe reconsider directly confronting them. In fact, even if you can, it's still not a good idea, because something Werewolf-side might notice you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Putting aside the benefits of Serpentis in VDA20 to reducing intimidation difficulties, one should think carefully about - and imagine accordingly for the game narrative - what it means for a character to have 10 dots in their Road and the merit Powerful Aura, reducing by 3 the difficulties of all interactions with humans plus all the attempts of a specific thing determined by the Road itself - in the case of the Road of Set in VDA20 it is unfortunately not written in the book due to some likely editing oversight, but at our table we agreed it's similar to how it is in VtM20: conversions. Plus religious actions and appearing as some divine manifestations instead of the true faith resistance bonus given in VtM20. I think it is perfectly legitimate by how the game is written that a vampire with such Road rating and merit, on this Road, for whom the most absurd and rarest (difficulty 10) of conversions become merely &quot;challenging&quot; (p.323, for difficulty 7 actions), may be one of those people that really turn around the lives of those who meet him. </div> </div> </div>Well, you know, all Garou are spiritual, but they can be quite deeply irreligious, especially Shadow Lords, who do not believe that they were literally born of Gaia and Grandfather Thunder to dominate so much as they feel it in their bones.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Don't quite see why. Humans rethink their existence quite often, and strong encounters play often a stronger role in this than long and slow conditioning. </div> </div> </div>Yes, but you see, you aren't dealing with the average human convert anymore...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Radical changes are rare either way, but so is meeting a vampire with what I have. Resistance to such changes is usually represented by Willpower and Conscience / Conviction (which Garou don't have). Willpower is ranked 1 to 10, with humans rarely having more than 5, and supernaturals having usually something more, though starting vampires and Garou alike won't have more than 5 without spending freebie points. </div> </div> </div>And they usually pump that shit up because the concepts demand it. To tell you the truth, it's a rather backward way of doing things, to be honest.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Garou have significantly shorter lifespans than vampires, so it's not a given that they will cultivate Willpower to its peak. Some will, most won't </div> </div> </div>Except the ones that are good at Facedowns, because the difficulty of a Facedown roll is the opponent's Willpower. Or the ones that investigate Wyrm Lore and don't break. To use another DBZA quote, makin' a lot of assumptions here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> . Even most vampires won't. </div> </div> </div>Vampires are spiritually dead. Technically, Garou can get equivalent ratings to various Virtues way, way higher than any vampire, because W:tDA places Courage as Rage or Willpower and Conscience/Conviction and Road as equal to Gnosis.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Garou's psychological resistance is mainly due to their Rage. Maybe it could be argued that in this scenario Gnosis might offer a bonus as it represents the lived and instinctual experience of the truth of Garou beliefs about the spirit world, but nobody is trying to convince them that the spirit world is false, so the relevance of Gnosis here is debatable. </div> </div> </div>But isn't that kind of the entire Setite thing? That the truths that they know aren't real?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Aside from mechanics, the game plot is full of supernaturals setting up cults around themselves, sometimes with unlikely followers. Other than their higher Willpower or specific powers, supernaturals have no special resistance to the fascination of religious beliefs or religious people, and even to changing them. Most of them either follow a human faith and are touched by the saints of said faiths, or they profess some supernatural faith and are touched by the &quot;pious&quot; of that faith, and may well rethink their beliefs if these are shaken by rare encounters. A vampire with 10 in any Road is a rare encounter, even if it's a 13th gen nobody for anything else, and a vampire with 10 in a Road that mystically empowers conversion - the Serpent was, after all, the top inspirer of doubt - is even rarer. Sure, unless there is work done later to consolidate and nurture faith, even the memory of such a rare encounter might well fade like a dream, but at least in the moment I don't think it's off the table that they feel overwhelmed by a fear they never felt before and bend the knee and avoid attacking the stranger who showed up in their midst after doing things others have not even dared before. </div> </div> </div>When that happens in Werewolf there's usually a tangible effect on the order of bane problems. Then it's not quite ghost bustin' time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I can't think of anything in the game system of any of the WoD splats that requires 50-100 successes. Is there some spell in M:tA perhaps that does? Conditioning, the 4th power of Dominate, requires 50 successes max, and is intended as a long term total mental control. Here we are talking about a single intense experience caused by 2 powers (Serpentis 1 and 3 of VDA20) and something rarer and truer than disciplines - high Road ratings, which have a powerful social effect premised on mastery of the Beast - something even many Methuselahs can't claim to have, and nobody is asking for total Dominate-like control. In principle, I'm asking not to be attacked, for which doubt is enough, or a superficial, not heartfelt, bending of the knee, though with 7 successes, against an optimised Garou in crinos, which may even exceed the Willpower of many of those present, I would easily justify things going even further. </div> </div> </div>Context matters; Garou tend to view words and random acts as empty air, so you'd need to be awfully persistent in order to be convincing. Note also that their Renown progression takes a shit load of work. When you get down to it, though, sucks to suck blood. Garou and Fera live in a world where Naturae approach them more readily than humans, and their own souls aren't withered, dead things with serious cases of degeneration. That's why becoming an Abomination is considered so much worse by them than for humans that become vampires.<br /> <br /> Also, the rite of caern building used to require a minimum of 40 successes (70 to get a Rating 5 caern, nearly impossible with the limited amount of rolls). Now it just takes a few successes. It still costs over a hundred points of Gnosis.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hey, I said I don't know W:tA well and that's why I asked. No need to be surprised by what I imagined, I never claimed to know how their relationship with Gaia or the Wyrm works. </div> </div> </div>Yet you're arguing that Garou are no different than humans even when presented with evidence to the contrary. Here's another major order of business - if you do something cruel, terrible, and all around shitty to a victim and a bane is around to see it, it makes things exponentially worse and so much more fucked up than if it went unnoticed. Garou can kill the banes in question, most of the time, meaning that they can kind of swerve around and keep within Gaia's graces without having their heads taken for a ride by a bane's mind games even when they fuck up. Every so often, though, they don't manage to do that, and that's generally what starts a fall, which, again, typically ends when the Garou flat out swears allegiance to a powerful agent of the enemy that's engaged via ritual means or they just dance the spiral. There's no progression meter for it, though. W:tA isn't really focused on personal horror, after all. It's splatter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Cool, so they probably would have massive bonuses to terrorise people upon sight, or probably people need a Courage roll just to look at them, and the Garou that manage to go against them would have higher resistance than that by whatever means the game system offers. Still doesn't change that Garou are not immune to fear or intimidation if they're not in rage or using special powers. </div> </div> </div>Actually, they use the Delirium rules against normal humans. That's based on the WP rating with a Wits + Occult roll to move up higher. I suppose that might seem frustratingly inconsistent from other games' POV.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T20:10:45-07:00","id":1524302,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, I pose a question to you - do you think that W:tA allows characters to use Strength + Intimidation alone to frighten others? Cut to the chase, the answer is no, it doesn't; even given the example of throwing someone across the room while in Crinos, it suggests a bonus to Manipulation + Intimidation, and you don't suffer the normal Crinos penalty to Manipulation. There are a few good reasons for this.<br /> <br /> Firstly, allowing Strength to be used in the dice pool would rather heavily flatten the curve of possible dice pools and encourage just shifting into Crinos or Hispo to make the roll. If you can change into Crinos, you always have a high dice pool at the ready, so you'd be looking at a rather homogeneous composition of competence for a pack, rather than having the Shadow Lords specialize in this kind of thing.<br /> <br /> Secondly, from a world-building perspective, Garou don't tend to be impressed by physical force alone. So you broke some stone work? Big deal! Any liquored up Garou could that that with Crinos at a construction site. If you don't have Renown, you are, at best, a nobody with potential. Just like how Status isn't just a simple matter of a Background with Garou, getting other Garou to back down is an elaborate system of mind games meant to hopefully curb the incidence of frenzied combats to the death. That said, if your Rage is too high and you're in a Facedown competition, you use that instead of Cha + Int, and rolling too many successes in one go will cause you to frenzy (and lose the contest, incidentally). Difficulty is adjusted upward for higher Rank opponents.</div> </div> </div> </div>We're not playing WtA20. We're playing VDA20 with werewolves statted according to WtA20. From VDA20 p.338, on how Intimidation works: &quot;Intimidation [Strength/ Manipulation + Intimidation] Intimidation has two effects. Passively, the higher your character\u2019s Intimidation rating, the wider the berth that others give him. Actively, intimidation works through subtlety or outright threat. Subtlety is based on a perceived threat. Roll Manipulation + Intimidation in a resisted action against the subject\u2019s Willpower (difficulty 6 for both rolls); the target must get more successes or be effectively cowed. The blatant form of intimidation involves direct physical threat. In this case, roll Strength + Intimidation in a resisted roll (difficulty 6) against the subject\u2019s Willpower or her Strength + Intimidation (whichever is higher). On a botch, your character looks patently ridiculous and doesn\u2019t impress anyone in attendance for the rest of the scene.&quot;<br /> <br /> WtA20 does not mention Strength+Intimidation in its mechanics, but it doesn't mention the resisted Willpower/Strength+Intimidation roll either. It also doesn't mention that the reason why the rolls are different from other splats is because werewolves are extra scary /un-scareable. It just says: &quot;Intimidation Even the most placid Child of Gaia can be one scary motherfucker. Though Garou lose Manipulation dice when they shift to different forms, this loss does not apply when the werewolf\u2019s trying to intimidate someone. In fact, she might actually gain dice, in any form, if she does something suitably impressive\u2026 like picking up a full-grown man and throwing him across the room. As an optional rule, a werewolf may automatically intimidate normal humans with a glance and a snarl, even in Homid form, if her Rage dice pool exceeds her target\u2019s Willpower. The target gets a look at the inner Beast, and while he might resist its effects with a successful Willpower roll (difficulty equal to the werewolf\u2019s Rage), he\u2019s freaked out nonetheless. Mages, ghouls, and so forth are made of sterner stuff \u2014 they might be impressed but not cowed. Crinos Garou, of course, automatically \u201cintimidate\u201d human beings. Even if the Delirium doesn\u2019t steal his sanity, it\u2019s safe to say that a person facing a raging war-wolf will feel intimidated.&quot;<br /> It doesn't even explain how werewolves intimidate each other. It does mention this as an example of roll on WtA20 p.240 however:<br /> &quot;You threaten the mouthy young pup by lifting him off the floor by his collar. Roll Strength + Intimidation (difficulty 8) to get him back in line.&quot;<br /> <br /> To me it seems evident that the system is not meant to be too different, but that the writing in vampire has been clearer on this issue. Either way, the game is VDA20 and the intimidator is a vampire, so it's ruled according to what's in the VDA20 book, and even if we wanted to go for the WtA20 book, there just aren't any rules for it there. <br /> <br /> As for the idea that Garou are not impressed by Strength, that makes no sense, and if it did, it would apply equally to most vampires once they are beyond a certain generation or level of Potence, since then they can easily have higher Strength (or successes on Strength rolls through Potence) than a Garou ever could in Crinos after just 55 xp, barring the Get of Fenris gift to double Strength for a few turns. I'm assuming, for testing purposes, that they both start with Strength 1, though unlikely for both. And after those 55 xp, the disparity between them in Strength only increases, and it gets worse if the vampire is of low gen (9th downward) and/or has Potence as an in-clan discipline.<br /> Simply, Garou, when they are in any form that gives them extra Strength, have more Strength than common and resist intimidation better in proportion to that Strength. Their Strength can still be surpassed by other Garou or other creatures or they may be intimidated before they transform in Glabro, Crinos or Hispo so that they do not feel the reassurance of their extra muscles. Fear is not a rational thing, and characters do not resist intimidation attempts by neatly thinking what powers and gifts they COULD activate to overcome the intimidator. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is an extreme system assumption mismatch. Basically, the action you used, even with Potence twice as high, still wouldn't have accrued that many successes because Garou only seem to respond to mind game-style Intimidation, and higher Rank Garou, as noted, are less susceptible to being cowed. Basically, you've gotten too hung up on technical difficulties and didn't take into account that the action wasn't just rare, it was flat out impossible. You didn't make a convincing display of force, you did the Garou equivalent of &quot;You got served&quot;. An actual display of physical force that would give the Garou pause for thought is generally only accomplished by thunderwyrms or zmei, both of which are not just possessed of high Strength ratings, they're also fucking huge and capable of crushing Garou, Fera, or vampires under their weight just by moving or falling. This is the world that they live in, and if you're not prepared to bring that to the table, maybe reconsider directly confronting them. In fact, even if you can, it's still not a good idea, because something Werewolf-side might notice you.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you incorrectly assumed that werewolves are immune to direct physical intimidation just because the WtA20 manual or whatever manual you use for reference happens not to explain in sufficient detail how intimidation works. Honestly, I haven't seen anywhere in the manual that Garou are immune to intimidation, at all. It doesn't even say under the rules for berserk frenzy, though it may be inferred by the fact that their brains are barely functioning, so they wouldn't actually understand that there is a threat. They actually have fox frenzy too (the fact that some may avoid it through gifts or powers is a special ability and not a universal Garou condition) and the list of reasons for Rage rolls on pp.261-2 of WtA20 clearly imply they can be scared, especially by a &quot;superior enemy&quot;, which is a consideration relative to the power of each individual Garou, not to the fact the Nation overall occasionally defeats huge beasts at massive life cost. I mean, by that reasoning, if werewolves aren't scared of vampires ever because some werewolves battle the Zmei (and most die in the attempt), vampires should not be afraid of werewolves because Baba Yaga - a Nosferatu Metuselah - brought the Zmei into the world and Vladimir Rustovich slaughtered werewolves for centuries. It's total nonsense. Everyone is intimidated according to their individual power level, not the whole of their splat. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, you know, all Garou are spiritual, but they can be quite deeply irreligious, especially Shadow Lords, who do not believe that they were literally born of Gaia and Grandfather Thunder to dominate so much as they feel it in their bones.</div> </div> </div> </div>Frankly irrelevant. And I have explained that I don't even care whether they become true believers in Set. Whether they're into religion or not, 7 successes will make anyone cow and bend the knee to whoever is scaring them to the bones.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, but you see, you aren't dealing with the average human convert anymore...</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And they usually pump that shit up because the concepts demand it. To tell you the truth, it's a rather backward way of doing things, to be honest.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Except the ones that are good at Facedowns, because the difficulty of a Facedown roll is the opponent's Willpower. Or the ones that investigate Wyrm Lore and don't break. To use another DBZA quote, makin' a lot of assumptions here.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's all somewhat not answering the point or addressed by other things I said later. It doesn't really matter whether they pump Willpower or not at the end of the day. And it does not matter that some do pump it for Facedowns or Wyrm Lore: most still don't. And I'm acting against a group, so the fact that a few of them are really good are facedowns or wyrm lore makes no difference. And the roll doesn't even involve Willpower at this point. And unless they have maxed out Strength, Intimidation AND Willpower, they're losing the contrasted roll by more than 7 successes, or the 7 successes match or are higher than their Willpower.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Vampires are spiritually dead. Technically, Garou can get equivalent ratings to various Virtues way, way higher than any vampire, because W:tDA places Courage as Rage or Willpower and Conscience/Conviction and Road as equal to Gnosis.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, doesn't change much.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But isn't that kind of the entire Setite thing? That the truths that they know aren't real?</div> </div> </div> </div>Setites don't have it in their doctrine to convince you that the spirit world isn't real. In fact, via Akhu they have access to the Spirit Manipulation path and occasionally cooperate with banes or other spirits. If anything, a Setite might consider pushing werwolves towards Harano as a way of calling into doubt the cause served by the Nation and its viability.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When that happens in Werewolf there's usually a tangible effect on the order of bane problems. Then it's not quite ghost bustin' time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not sure what you meant here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Context matters; Garou tend to view words and random acts as empty air, so you'd need to be awfully persistent in order to be convincing. Note also that their Renown progression takes a shit load of work. When you get down to it, though, sucks to suck blood. Garou and Fera live in a world where Naturae approach them more readily than humans, and their own souls aren't withered, dead things with serious cases of degeneration. That's why becoming an Abomination is considered so much worse by them than for humans that become vampires.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly it seems you're assuming more about Garou psychology than the manuals actually say and rule. They're strong, yes, they're cool dudes, but they're not invincible, they're not immune to mind games, they're not immune to actually many things. They have some traits that tend to be higher for them than for others, such as starting Strength once you turn into Crinos, and this will help them with some rolls and put them above the average, but they can still find people with higher traits who beat them even where their strength is. It's actually interesting how from the beginning of our exchange you hardly ever mentioned an actual rule, and simply made statements about the fluff and lore and how it should make Garou immune to this and that. It's like saying that, given how hyped elder vampires are as super dangerous, there should be nothing in a game that defeats them. Nonsense. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, the rite of caern building used to require a minimum of 40 successes (70 to get a Rating 5 caern, nearly impossible with the limited amount of rolls). Now it just takes a few successes. It still costs over a hundred points of Gnosis.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right. A ritual that's rarely performed and super dangerous. And hardly comparable to force converting people.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yet you're arguing that Garou are no different than humans even when presented with evidence to the contrary.</div> </div> </div> </div>But I haven't seen any evidence. I just read people saying Garou can't be scared because they're bred to do bigger things. As if that meant anything, and as if there were no actual rules to figure out how different situations could play out depending on the individuals involved and the circumstances. Can anyone bring me the quote from the WtA20 manual that says Garou can't be intimidated and would not be cowed into something crazy if the opponent scores a ton of successes against the best that the Nation has to offer?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524296#post1524296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, they use the Delirium rules against normal humans. That's based on the WP rating with a Wits + Occult roll to move up higher. I suppose that might seem frustratingly inconsistent from other games' POV.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you mean that stuff like Nexus Crawlers afflicts Garou with Delirium like Garou afflict humans with Delirium? Cool. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T20:35:36-07:00","id":1524303,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524302#post1524302\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> We're not playing WtA20. We're playing VDA20 with werewolves statted according to WtA20. From VDA20 p.338, on how Intimidation works: &quot;Intimidation [Strength/ Manipulation + Intimidation] Intimidation has two effects. Passively, the higher your character\u2019s Intimidation rating, the wider the berth that others give him. Actively, intimidation works through subtlety or outright threat. Subtlety is based on a perceived threat. Roll Manipulation + Intimidation in a resisted action against the subject\u2019s Willpower (difficulty 6 for both rolls); the target must get more successes or be effectively cowed. The blatant form of intimidation involves direct physical threat. In this case, roll Strength + Intimidation in a resisted roll (difficulty 6) against the subject\u2019s Willpower or her Strength + Intimidation (whichever is higher). On a botch, your character looks patently ridiculous and doesn\u2019t impress anyone in attendance for the rest of the scene.&quot;<br /> <br /> WtA20 does not mention Strength+Intimidation in its mechanics, but it doesn't mention the resisted Willpower/Strength+Intimidation roll either. It also doesn't mention that the reason why the rolls are different from other splats is because werewolves are extra scary /un-scareable. It just says: &quot;Intimidation Even the most placid Child of Gaia can be one scary motherfucker. Though Garou lose Manipulation dice when they shift to different forms, this loss does not apply when the werewolf\u2019s trying to intimidate someone. In fact, she might actually gain dice, in any form, if she does something suitably impressive\u2026 like picking up a full-grown man and throwing him across the room. As an optional rule, a werewolf may automatically intimidate normal humans with a glance and a snarl, even in Homid form, if her Rage dice pool exceeds her target\u2019s Willpower. The target gets a look at the inner Beast, and while he might resist its effects with a successful Willpower roll (difficulty equal to the werewolf\u2019s Rage), he\u2019s freaked out nonetheless. Mages, ghouls, and so forth are made of sterner stuff \u2014 they might be impressed but not cowed. Crinos Garou, of course, automatically \u201cintimidate\u201d human beings. Even if the Delirium doesn\u2019t steal his sanity, it\u2019s safe to say that a person facing a raging war-wolf will feel intimidated.&quot;<br /> It doesn't even explain how werewolves intimidate each other. It does mention this as an example of roll on WtA20 p.240 however:<br /> &quot;You threaten the mouthy young pup by lifting him off the floor by his collar. Roll Strength + Intimidation (difficulty 8) to get him back in line.&quot;<br /> <br /> To me it seems evident that the system is not meant to be too different, but that the writing in vampire has been clearer on this issue. Either way, the game is VDA20 and the intimidator is a vampire, so it's ruled according to what's in the VDA20 book, and even if we wanted to go for the WtA20 book, there just aren't any rules for it there.<br /> <br /> As for the idea that Garou are not impressed by Strength, that makes no sense, and if it did, it would apply equally to most vampires once they are beyond a certain generation or level of Potence, since then they can easily have higher Strength (or successes on Strength rolls through Potence) than a Garou ever could in Crinos after just 55 xp, barring the Get of Fenris gift to double Strength for a few turns. I'm assuming, for testing purposes, that they both start with Strength 1, though unlikely for both. And after those 55 xp, the disparity between them in Strength only increases, and it gets worse if the vampire is of low gen (9th downward) and/or has Potence as an in-clan discipline.<br /> Simply, Garou, when they are in any form that gives them extra Strength, have more Strength than common and resist intimidation better in proportion to that Strength. Their Strength can still be surpassed by other Garou or other creatures or they may be intimidated before they transform in Glabro, Crinos or Hispo so that they do not feel the reassurance of their extra muscles. Fear is not a rational thing, and characters do not resist intimidation attempts by neatly thinking what powers and gifts they COULD activate to overcome the intimidator.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I think you incorrectly assumed that werewolves are immune to direct physical intimidation just because the WtA20 manual or whatever manual you use for reference happens not to explain in sufficient detail how intimidation works. Honestly, I haven't seen anywhere in the manual that Garou are immune to intimidation, at all. It doesn't even say under the rules for berserk frenzy, though it may be inferred by the fact that their brains are barely functioning, so they wouldn't actually understand that there is a threat. They actually have fox frenzy too (the fact that some may avoid it through gifts or powers is a special ability and not a universal Garou condition) and the list of reasons for Rage rolls on pp.261-2 of WtA20 clearly imply they can be scared, especially by a &quot;superior enemy&quot;, which is a consideration relative to the power of each individual Garou, not to the fact the Nation overall occasionally defeats huge beasts at massive life cost. I mean, by that reasoning, if werewolves aren't scared of vampires ever because some werewolves battle the Zmei (and most die in the attempt), vampires should not be afraid of werewolves because Baba Yaga - a Nosferatu Metuselah - brought the Zmei into the world and Vladimir Rustovich slaughtered werewolves for centuries. It's total nonsense. Everyone is intimidated according to their individual power level, not the whole of their splat.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Frankly irrelevant. And I have explained that I don't even care whether they become true believers in Set. Whether they're into religion or not, 7 successes will make anyone cow and bend the knee to whoever is scaring them to the bones.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> That's all somewhat not answering the point or addressed by other things I said later. It doesn't really matter whether they pump Willpower or not at the end of the day. And it does not matter that some do pump it for Facedowns or Wyrm Lore: most still don't. And I'm acting against a group, so the fact that a few of them are really good are facedowns or wyrm lore makes no difference. And the roll doesn't even involve Willpower at this point. And unless they have maxed out Strength, Intimidation AND Willpower, they're losing the contrasted roll by more than 7 successes, or the 7 successes match or are higher than their Willpower.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Again, doesn't change much.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Setites don't have it in their doctrine to convince you that the spirit world isn't real. In fact, via Akhu they have access to the Spirit Manipulation path and occasionally cooperate with banes or other spirits. If anything, a Setite might consider pushing werwolves towards Harano as a way of calling into doubt the cause served by the Nation and its viability.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Not sure what you meant here.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Honestly it seems you're assuming more about Garou psychology than the manuals actually say and rule. They're strong, yes, they're cool dudes, but they're not invincible, they're not immune to mind games, they're not immune to actually many things. They have some traits that tend to be higher for them than for others, such as starting Strength once you turn into Crinos, and this will help them with some rolls and put them above the average, but they can still find people with higher traits who beat them even where their strength is. It's actually interesting how from the beginning of our exchange you hardly ever mentioned an actual rule, and simply made statements about the fluff and lore and how it should make Garou immune to this and that. It's like saying that, given how hyped elder vampires are as super dangerous, there should be nothing in a game that defeats them. Nonsense.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Right. A ritual that's rarely performed and super dangerous. And hardly comparable to force converting people.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> But I haven't seen any evidence.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>You don't want to. The notion of physical intimidation using Strength is so ingrained in your assumptions that you're not willing to question its underpinnings, even when Iexplained that Garou, being shapeshifters who can hulk out, don't really put much stock in that, or that they have a system of face that makes most of the assumptions of Vampire foreign and, to be quite frank, nonsensical.<br /> <br /> Enjoy playing Vampire, dunno why you asked to be told about Werewolf if you weren't going to use it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T20:45:35-07:00","id":1524304,"title":"","content":"While in theory you could convert a werewolf to replace their faith in Gaia for Set, it would take an extended series of rolls over a fairly long amount of time and likely require a significant amount of physical and mental torture. This is often what happens when Black Spirals try to force Gaian Garou to begin following the Wyrm. It's something that happens, but it's by no means easy at all. A single success might cow them for a few moments. <i>Maybe</i> they could say, &quot;Sure, yeah Set's the guy,&quot; but then they'd tear you apart a few moments later when you let your guard down.<br /> <br /> Unlike humans, werewolves are very familiar with the supernatural, many of them would prefer to die before turning their back on Gaia, and they all know about Set. In fact if there's any Silent Striders among the werewolves, they well might frenzy and start attacking you. <br /> <br /> So it might be a useful short term strategy to throw them off guard momentarily, but I'd suggest getting the heck out of there, and don't expect that you'll have a loyal pack of werewolves following you around from now on. Unless that's the direction your ST wants to take things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-30T23:29:14-07:00","id":1524793,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524304#post1524304\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While in theory you could convert a werewolf to replace their faith in Gaia for Set, it would take an extended series of rolls over a fairly long amount of time and likely require a significant amount of physical and mental torture. This is often what happens when Black Spirals try to force Gaian Garou to begin following the Wyrm. It's something that happens, but it's by no means easy at all. A single success might cow them for a few moments. <i>Maybe</i> they could say, &quot;Sure, yeah Set's the guy,&quot; but then they'd tear you apart a few moments later when you let your guard down.<br /> <br /> Unlike humans, werewolves are very familiar with the supernatural, many of them would prefer to die before turning their back on Gaia, and they all know about Set. In fact if there's any Silent Striders among the werewolves, they well might frenzy and start attacking you.<br /> <br /> So it might be a useful short term strategy to throw them off guard momentarily, but I'd suggest getting the heck out of there, and don't expect that you'll have a loyal pack of werewolves following you around from now on. Unless that's the direction your ST wants to take things.</div> </div> </div> </div>You mentioned &quot;a single success might cow them for a few moments&quot;. Yeah, for a single success I would not expect anything more. In fact, with 3 successes, which is the VDA20 standard of managing to do what you were trying to do, I would only expect them to surrender without necessarily converting even outwardly. It would be like &quot;ok, Set-guy. Seen as you have a Strength way beyond the 10 dots that is the legal maximum of all our known antagonists except vampires (because with Potence and blood expenditure even a 13th gen vamp could theoretically go to Strength 10 for a few turns plus 5 dots of Potence) - fomori have max Strength 10 even with their powers, according to WtA20; Black Spiral Dancers I think get to 9 dots max in crinos like normal Garou unless some gift like Might of Thor temporarily boosts that; spirits have a max of Rage 10, even Nexus Crawlers, and I get that they have other powers, but not everything is calculated in intimidation, and some things have already clarified effects like causing Delirium in humans -, it seems wise to nominally surrender and let you go, but don't push it.&quot; But the issue in the original post was that the vampire in questions had an average chance of scoring 7 successes beyond those scored by a Garou with Strength 9 in Crinos and Intimidate 5 with specialisation in physical coercion. <br /> So the question would need to be reframed: what on earth happens to enemies who are cowed not by 1 success, but by 7, and that's counting their max stats (though they could have some extra dice or difficulty changes with some totems or gifts or fetish, but that would make a limited difference in terms of successes)?<br /> <br /> I'm also gonna ask your opinion on the discussion between me and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23\" data-vbnamecard=\"23\">Saur Ops Specialist</a>: knowing that WtA20 does not mention for Garou the Strength+Intimidation roll in the systems and drama chapter but it does mention it in an example roll earlier in the book, do you think that is enough to argue that werewolves are just outright immune to this form of intimidation which is basic in other splats? Or do you think that's just an editing issue, and it would have made more sense to mention this type of roll also in the Systems and Drama chapter?<br /> And also, do you think werewolves, just by being werewolves, get bonuses on such a roll (which other supernaturals don't get) or do they just get the benefits already implicit in their powers, ex.: they naturally have higher Strength scores when they transform in glabro, hispo and crinos and higher Willpower than common humans, so that already buffs them up in resisted intimidation rolls compared to others?<br /> <br /> Mind you that, yes, they are brought up to hunt spirits, fomori and the occasional vampire, but the WtA20 book still describe vampires and their powers, especially ancients (7th gen downward, judging by the blood pool given in the stats) as menacing antagonists. Consider that a low gen vampire can easily have physical attributes higher than 10 (the max for spirits and fomori) thanks to Potence, Fortitude and Celerity and blood spending, and in VDA20 there is no actual limit to how much you can raise your attributes as long as you keep spending blood every turn. \u0305","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T01:19:06-07:00","id":1524795,"title":"","content":"With all due respect your plan was &quot;to try intimidating them into surrendering to Set or being exterminated before they go into frenzy&quot;<br /> Neither would work<br /> Remember, Fear is knowing you're in a monster-filled forest. Terror is seeing one run at you. Horror is realizing your feet are glued to the ground.<br /> Showing up on their doorstep and presenting your might or even divinity of Set (let's say with Serpentis, the LvL 9 Godhead, or LVL8 Body of the Great Lord Set or maybe with LvL 7 Form of the Storm - a bit of a stretch but Seth was the God of Storms, so... maaaaybe? But similarly LvL 7 Divine Image would work better) is enough to be killed by a pack of furrballs<br /> \u200bEven if for some reason you managed to have Daimonion and use a clever combination of Sense the Sin, Fear of the Void Below and Psychomachia wouldn't be enough to pull thrugh what you want<br /> You simply <i>can't be more terrifying</i> <i>and dreadfull</i> than they face day-to-day - and certanly not enough to shake their fate<br /> You wouldn't even be on the level of hearing the distant roar of a Thunderwyrm - that usually means a good chunk of a cityblock going to be leveled. And that is just the distant sound of it!<br /> Well you can, but can't hold the candle to the horrors what they encounter from since they are young teens - and those job is to be horrible! - even with supernatural backup<br /> What you want <i>maybe</i> would be enough to send them in to frenzy - let's put aside the fact, you mentioned they are Shadowlords who to have learn a good portion of their tricks have to deal with <i>Fear</i>-spirits, Fear <i>itself</i><br /> To scare and intimidate with a display of power a Garou - lets say just one, not the whole team - to even doubt for a single moment \u200bthey are on the wrong side and to doubt Gaia's will?<br /> Yeah, sorry, no amount of succes will do that<br /> What <b>AnubisXy</b>\u200b said with the extended series of rolls over a fairly long amount of time and likely require a significant amount of physical and mental torture, or slowly erroding away their faith in theory could work - tho' probably not with the display of power or similar crude method - but what you described?<br /> No<br /> I doubt you could find a ST (except if not familar with Werewolf and use Luppines) who would say, okay... you could do what you described<br /> <br /> But of course this is just my two cents, and could be wrong, just my opinion in the question","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T01:47:48-07:00","id":1524796,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524795#post1524795\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With all due respect your plan was &quot;to try intimidating them into surrendering to Set or being exterminated before they go into frenzy&quot;<br /> Neither would work<br /> Remember, Fear is knowing you're in a monster-filled forest. Terror is seeing one run at you. Horror is realizing your feet are glued to the ground.<br /> Showing up on their doorstep and presenting your might or even divinity of Set (let's say with Serpentis, the LvL 9 Godhead, or LVL8 Body of the Great Lord Set or maybe with LvL 7 Form of the Storm - a bit of a stretch but Seth was the God of Storms, so... maaaaybe? But similarly LvL 7 Divine Image would work better) is enough to be killed by a pack of furrballs<br /> \u200bEven if for some reason you managed to have Daimonion and use a clever combination of Sense the Sin, Fear of the Void Below and Psychomachia wouldn't be enough to pull thrugh what you want<br /> You simply <i>can't be more terrifying</i> <i>and dreadfull</i> than they face day-to-day - and certanly not enough to shake their fate<br /> You wouldn't even be on the level of hearing the distant roar of a Thunderwyrm - that usually means a good chunk of a cityblock going to be leveled. And that is just the distant sound of it!<br /> Well you can, but can't hold the candle to the horrors what they encounter from since they are young teens - and those job is to be horrible! - even with supernatural backup<br /> What you want <i>maybe</i> would be enough to send them in to frenzy - let's put aside the fact, you mentioned they are Shadowlords who to have learn a good portion of their tricks have to deal with <i>Fear</i>-spirits, Fear <i>itself</i><br /> To scare and intimidate with a display of power a Garou - lets say just one, not the whole team - to even doubt for a single moment \u200bthey are on the wrong side and to doubt Gaia's will?<br /> Yeah, sorry, no amount of succes will do that<br /> What <b>AnubisXy</b>\u200b said with the extended series of rolls over a fairly long amount of time and likely require a significant amount of physical and mental torture, or slowly erroding away their faith in theory could work - tho' probably not with the display of power or similar crude method - but what you described?<br /> No<br /> I doubt you could find a ST (except if not familar with Werewolf and use Luppines) who would say, okay... you could do what you described<br /> <br /> But of course this is just my two cents, and could be wrong, just my opinion in the question</div> </div> </div> </div>Cool. So what would you rule with 7 successes? \u0130t's still an intimidation roll, it still needs to have consequence, it still says the loser of the resisted roll by 1 success is cowed and these are 7.<br /> I don't remember if \u0130 explained why it comes to 7 in the OP, but it's because of the combined effects of VDA20 Serpentis 1 (-1 diff on all social rolls) and 3 (-2 diff on intimidation rolls) plus a Road score of 10 with the merit Powerful Aura (-3 diff, applicable to this roll based on how the ST and \u0130 houseruled the aura of the Road of Set in VDA20 because it's not given in the book), so a total of -6 difficulty to the roll, plus Strength 12 (for the tail of Serpentis 4, which \u0130 slam on the ground as \u0130 threaten them to surrender to Set, otherwise 9) and Potence 5 (activated) at the moment of rolling, and 3 dots in \u0130ntimidation. So it's the melding of 4 powers (Serpentis 1, 3 and 4 + Potence) and the Road's bearing, plus blood pumping, that do this.<br /> \u0130 am ok with them not actually converting to Set. \u0130 am ok with some rolling Rage because triggered, though it would make more sense for me if it was for fox frenzy rather than berserker frenzy. And it's even better for me if they frenzy, because then they can't use gifts or pack tactics or combat maneuvers and my Stamina's (soaks aggravated damage thanks to Serpentis 3) pumped enough to average more successes than their average damage, even if there are a ton of attacks.<br /> But what do they do beyond that? How do those who don't frenzy react to 7 successes beyond theirs?<br /> \u0130f you were STing a werewolf game and the enemy did this, what would you tell to your players who have failed the resisted roll by 7 successes?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T02:38:35-07:00","id":1524798,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524795#post1524795\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With all due respect your plan was &quot;to try intimidating them into surrendering to Set or being exterminated before they go into frenzy&quot;<br /> Neither would work<br /> Remember, Fear is knowing you're in a monster-filled forest. Terror is seeing one run at you. Horror is realizing your feet are glued to the ground.<br /> Showing up on their doorstep and presenting your might or even divinity of Set (let's say with Serpentis, the LvL 9 Godhead, or LVL8 Body of the Great Lord Set or maybe with LvL 7 Form of the Storm - a bit of a stretch but Seth was the God of Storms, so... maaaaybe? But similarly LvL 7 Divine Image would work better) is enough to be killed by a pack of furrballs<br /> \u200bEven if for some reason you managed to have Daimonion and use a clever combination of Sense the Sin, Fear of the Void Below and Psychomachia wouldn't be enough to pull thrugh what you want<br /> You simply <i>can't be more terrifying</i> <i>and dreadfull</i> than they face day-to-day - and certanly not enough to shake their fate<br /> You wouldn't even be on the level of hearing the distant roar of a Thunderwyrm - that usually means a good chunk of a cityblock going to be leveled. And that is just the distant sound of it!<br /> Well you can, but can't hold the candle to the horrors what they encounter from since they are young teens - and those job is to be horrible! - even with supernatural backup<br /> What you want <i>maybe</i> would be enough to send them in to frenzy - let's put aside the fact, you mentioned they are Shadowlords who to have learn a good portion of their tricks have to deal with <i>Fear</i>-spirits, Fear <i>itself</i><br /> To scare and intimidate with a display of power a Garou - lets say just one, not the whole team - to even doubt for a single moment \u200bthey are on the wrong side and to doubt Gaia's will?<br /> Yeah, sorry, no amount of succes will do that<br /> What <b>AnubisXy</b>\u200b said with the extended series of rolls over a fairly long amount of time and likely require a significant amount of physical and mental torture, or slowly erroding away their faith in theory could work - tho' probably not with the display of power or similar crude method - but what you described?<br /> No<br /> I doubt you could find a ST (except if not familar with Werewolf and use Luppines) who would say, okay... you could do what you described<br /> <br /> But of course this is just my two cents, and could be wrong, just my opinion in the question</div> </div> </div> </div>Also (following from other comment): to me, when you and others mention their being werewolves who fight monsters daily, or being in their home, or being numerically superior, or being Shadow Lords and therefore familiar with Fear spirits...<br /> \u0130 think those are almost all sound considerations, but \u0130 would just put a number to them and factor them in the resisted roll. That, by itself, would change the question because there would not be 7 successes anymore.<br /> \u0130 am still not inclined to give an unwritten bonus to \u0130ntimidation rolls to werewolves in general or to Shadow Lords in particular, because something like that would have been written clearly in the published rules if it was meant to apply. But \u0130'm cool with bonuses for being on your turf or more numerous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T03:48:43-07:00","id":1524802,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524796#post1524796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u0130f you were STing a werewolf game and the enemy did this, what would you tell to your players who have failed the resisted roll by 7 successes?</div> </div> </div> </div>In that hypothetical scenario - tho' my Players know better to try such thing - I would politely remind them, the Game is more than numbers, dices and rules and sometimes common sense is enough instead of rolls (or &quot;overwrite&quot; them)<br /> But as I said, that is just me and my oppinion<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T08:46:52-07:00","id":1524807,"title":"","content":"There's also a simple problem with most iterations of the ST system: what 6+ success means in most rolls is completely in ST fiat territory. We're given a spectrum of success from 1 (minimal) to 5 (complete and then some). Outside of combat and similar subsystems where there's a specific role for each success, there's just a superfluousness to trying t adjudicate that many successes in most situations. If a character is picking a lock, what does 7 successes mean that 5 successes doesn't already include? <br /> <br /> Beyond that, I think two things need to be addressed First, is that mundane (even if modified by supernatural power) rolls aren't mind control. Individuals are going to react differently to the same thing. This is where a lot of the &quot;Garou shouldn't care&quot; sentiment is probably coming from. The Garou train to fight huge powerful monsters, so a threat display by a huge powerful monster isn't going to provoke the same reaction from them as it would a bunch of normal humans. The Garou also don't know your character's sheet, they don't know your Stamina, or that you have a power to soak high amounts of Agg damage. What they do, it dependent on my second issue:<br /> <br /> So, secondly, what is your characters intent for their roll? Is it reasonable for 3-5 seconds worth of time (convert to worshiping Set is not IMO), since smashing shit to show how dangerous you re is provoking an action time scale interaction? Is it reasonable for your methods (using Strength for intimidation attempts for that juicy Potence bonus comes with an inherent difference in what kind of threats you can make)?<br /> <br /> A big part of adjudicating what successes mean in the more vague areas of the rules, starts with what the intent of the roll is in the first place, and making sure the player and ST are on the same page. The ST has the right to say things like, &quot;that intent isn't appropriate for this context, please refine/rephrase it,&quot; If you want results that you'd get out of using Manipulation, but you insist on using Strength, it's completely within the ST's bailiwick to request you change things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T13:16:36-07:00","id":1524828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524807#post1524807\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's also a simple problem with most iterations of the ST system: what 6+ success means in most rolls is completely in ST fiat territory. We're given a spectrum of success from 1 (minimal) to 5 (complete and then some). Outside of combat and similar subsystems where there's a specific role for each success, there's just a superfluousness to trying t adjudicate that many successes in most situations. If a character is picking a lock, what does 7 successes mean that 5 successes doesn't already include?<br /> <br /> Beyond that, I think two things need to be addressed First, is that mundane (even if modified by supernatural power) rolls aren't mind control. Individuals are going to react differently to the same thing. This is where a lot of the &quot;Garou shouldn't care&quot; sentiment is probably coming from. The Garou train to fight huge powerful monsters, so a threat display by a huge powerful monster isn't going to provoke the same reaction from them as it would a bunch of normal humans. The Garou also don't know your character's sheet, they don't know your Stamina, or that you have a power to soak high amounts of Agg damage. What they do, it dependent on my second issue:<br /> <br /> So, secondly, what is your characters intent for their roll? Is it reasonable for 3-5 seconds worth of time (convert to worshiping Set is not IMO), since smashing shit to show how dangerous you re is provoking an action time scale interaction? Is it reasonable for your methods (using Strength for intimidation attempts for that juicy Potence bonus comes with an inherent difference in what kind of threats you can make)?<br /> <br /> A big part of adjudicating what successes mean in the more vague areas of the rules, starts with what the intent of the roll is in the first place, and making sure the player and ST are on the same page. The ST has the right to say things like, &quot;that intent isn't appropriate for this context, please refine/rephrase it,&quot; If you want results that you'd get out of using Manipulation, but you insist on using Strength, it's completely within the ST's bailiwick to request you change things.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a very thoughtful and agreeable answer, and \u0130 also find it much better to say, as ST: &quot;ok, the way you framed your intention does not work with the roll, but it could be changed in this way&quot; rather than simply &quot;no, this can never happen ever even if the manual makes it look so&quot;.<br /> <br /> Since part of my bonuses (-3 to difficulty) comes from the Road's bearing related to conversions, devotional work and appearing as divine, my intention is to demand outward present surrender to Set, whilst fully knowing that would be nothing other than a temporary fa\u00e7ade until \u0130 leave.<br /> \u0130 understand that it would normally be easier to say &quot;stay back and don't dare coming near&quot; than &quot;surrender to the might of my god&quot;, but in my case \u0130 get -3 difficulty by saying the latter, so in my specific case this is easier. The ultimate aim is basically to leave alive, as an escape option in case they find me. Whilst my Stamina can take a lot, Garou can also summon fire damage in various ways, which would kill me very quickly, and some soak rolls may be unlucky.<br /> <br /> What prompted the post is the fact that when \u0130 did the maths \u0130 realised the averaging of 7 success beyond theirs and \u0130 found it difficult, for the reasons you mentioned of how the ST system works, to imagine what would happen. \u0130f 5 successes mean great and lasting results, do 7 successes indicate a deeper surrender than hoped for, a conversion, or at least an exceptionally long lasting fear? \u0130t's down to the ST, of course.<br /> <br /> At any rate, when this thing actually happened in the last session (my Obfuscate was busted so \u0130 tried \u0130ntimidation out) it caused a big fuss because my ST was not open to it working as \u0130 said at all (not even to open a discussion over alternative interpretations or intentions), which got compounded with some other issues. Next game is next week and we 'll see what happens. My char and two more are in the caern with 240+ Garou and the ST has declared the characters already dead. He offered the option of doing the rolls though it would be a waste of time. \u0130 waved the right to the rolls and made a new character sheet, but voiced privately to him some issues \u0130 had with how things had gone down, since the character was dead anyway, and the ST now feels that we have to do the rolls because he won't tolerate my doubting the fact that the character would die, due to him being generally unhappy with how players have been playing lately, taking greater risks than usual and interpreting powers as stronger than he reads them.<br /> As far as \u0130'm concerned, if he uses fire elementals or an incarna or other means to inflict decent fire or Sun damage, \u0130'm defo dead. \u0130 can't see anything else in the WtA20 manual that would make me conclude that \u0130 have zero chance, because \u0130 can Stamina-lock them into doing zero damage statistically whilst clinching one with Potence one turn and drinking him dry the next to keep pumping Strength and Stamina and reactivating Serpentis 2 and 3 if the Philodox use Take the True Form. But it's objectively boring and luck-sensitive and can easily go wrong, \u0130 don't mind at all skipping it and using the new character. More than rolling, it seems the ST and the players are due a chat on the system and powers and rule interpretation.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T13:27:46-07:00","id":1524829,"title":"","content":"Did you know the numbers beforehand? Because a vampire trying to march up to a sept of that size is... wow. A deathwish doesn't even begin to describe it. There was probably an entire pack waiting to take shots at you with fetish bows that fire projectiles that burrow toward your heart (an actual thing, yes). They certainly have the numbers to dedicate a pack of that sort to defense, and also another to negotiate and bind spirits at the behest of the Ritemaster. The caern itself is probably a rather high rating, too, so the spiritual backup would be considerable and the caern's patron would likely be boosting them up for the occasion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T14:54:35-07:00","id":1524842,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524829#post1524829\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Did you know the numbers beforehand? Because a vampire trying to march up to a sept of that size is... wow. A deathwish doesn't even begin to describe it. There was probably an entire pack waiting to take shots at you with fetish bows that fire projectiles that burrow toward your heart (an actual thing, yes). They certainly have the numbers to dedicate a pack of that sort to defense, and also another to negotiate and bind spirits at the behest of the Ritemaster. The caern itself is probably a rather high rating, too, so the spiritual backup would be considerable and the caern's patron would likely be boosting them up for the occasion.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, no, I didn't know the numbers. To my understanding I wasn't even going to the caern itself, but to the adjacent area, obfuscated and also sneaking about mundanely just to be safe, with a magic item like a compass that told me what directions the Garou were in. I first explored the perimeter, then retreated, then went back to kill them in stealth, being specialised in that kind of thing and being able to dish out, in a surprise attack, enough damage to kill in one shot.<br /> <br /> Roleplaying the thing took several sessions because we play slow and the coterie was in two different places. When we started, I didn't actually know much about Werewolves game-wise. I've been reading up on them out of curiosity lately. In game, my only encounter with a Garou (rank 3 Get of Fenris) ended with me killing him in 1 turn without a scratch, so my character was quite justified to underestimate the threat.<br /> <br /> And in all cases, I did not know they were going to be 240 werewolves (not lupines + kinfolk), including several rank 5 and rank 6, by what the ST said to ensure we understand we're dead. He doesn't normally storytell werewolves, btw, so he's also been catching up on their powers. For example, he keeps referring to them as a &quot;pack&quot; and has not used the word sept, which would make more sense for a caern and for those numbers, so I'm not sure he's read yet in the book what the difference is. I don't even know if 240+ is a normal number even for a big caern, and I thought rank 5 and rank 6 werewolves were rare, like 1, 2 or 3 in a whole sept.<br /> <br /> Of course, in retrospect I now know that 1) Obfuscate and Stealth are not enough to stay hidden from werewolves on their own; 2) if I ever do a mission like that again (unlikely), I will start killing stealthily all I see from the get go, rather than letting them figure out I'm there hiding somewhere and use their time to prepare their defenses.<br /> <br /> In principle, I was not planning on being discovered, thought neither player nor character had a clear idea of Garou sensing capacities. I learned that later, did the maths on the difficulty 8 Sensing aganst Obfuscate, and figured out that I averaged more successes in the resisted roll than a lupus with amplified senses, Pereption 5 and Primal Urge 5. Unfortunately, the ST didn't even roll/let me roll for that. To my understanding, he mainly relied on Sense the Wyrm, the fact that I left tracks on the ground despite being stealthy and obfuscated, and spirit tracking.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":16965,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","user":"Hermeticus","date":". 10-31-2023, 06:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T16:29:42-07:00","id":1524849,"title":"","content":"I think you may have some details to iron out with your ST that go a bit beyond use of werewolves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T22:43:12-07:00","id":1524860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524849#post1524849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you may have some details to iron out with your ST that go a bit beyond use of werewolves.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I think so too. Not sure if you mean it the same way as I understand it, but yeah, on my side I find his playstyle a bit hard to deal with because I understand many rules or powers differently and also since I joined the playing group the chronicle has kept us prisoners of this or that PNG for most sessions, and when we were not actively imprisoned, we had one reason or another to have to do what they wanted. I did play along, because the circumstances still made sense narratively, but I do find that heavy handed approach a bit frustrating, and locking us up if we ever do something a bit carelessly (a carelessness which, by the way, I would personally handle with rolls, after interpretation) or daringly. \u0130'm more into pursuing the characters' ambitions and letting them lead the story. \u0130 also noticed from the older players a strange lack of planning or taking stock of what their characters can do... and they've been playing decades longer than me. When \u0130 hear them speak, sometimes they talk of how the ST plans the stories and how to find the solution that the ST has already planned or envisioned, as if it was a guess game where they need to find the right answer concealed by the ST. On his side, the ST also said he's getting frustrated with how some players are playing or interpreting powers.<br /> \u0130'm relatively new to the group, but, yeah, it seems like we need to sit down and figure out what's going on, regardless of Setites wandering into caerns.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-31T23:25:37-07:00","id":1524863,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524793#post1524793\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So the question would need to be reframed: what on earth happens to enemies who are cowed not by 1 success, but by 7, and that's counting their max stats (though they could have some extra dice or difficulty changes with some totems or gifts or fetish, but that would make a limited difference in terms of successes)?</div> </div> </div> </div>It would say many of them piss their pants. And I'd roll to see if any of them frenzy in fear and run away. With luck there might only be one or two left standing there. I wouldn't have them immediately kneel and say, &quot;Hail Set&quot; or anything, even if you got 1000 successes.<br /> <br /> Ultimately Attribue+Ability rolls aren't magical - even if you roll 1000 successes on a Computers roll, you're not instantly hacking the Pentagon in 5 seconds. Likewise, even with 1000 successes on an Intimidation check, you're not going to be instantly overwrite people's deeply held held beliefs like that. If you want to do soemthing like that, it would require a number of rolls over a period of time, or if you want to bypass the time, that would require the use of magic (ie Disciplines) to do so.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-01T01:33:34-07:00","id":1524865,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524863#post1524863\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It would say many of them piss their pants. And I'd roll to see if any of them frenzy in fear and run away. With luck there might only be one or two left standing there. I wouldn't have them immediately kneel and say, &quot;Hail Set&quot; or anything, even if you got 1000 successes.<br /> <br /> Ultimately Attribue+Ability rolls aren't magical - even if you roll 1000 successes on a Computers roll, you're not instantly hacking the Pentagon in 5 seconds. Likewise, even with 1000 successes on an Intimidation check, you're not going to be instantly overwrite people's deeply held held beliefs like that. If you want to do soemthing like that, it would require a number of rolls over a period of time, or if you want to bypass the time, that would require the use of magic (ie Disciplines) to do so.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds reasonable and also works fine for me. \u0130 did my job - calling them to Set's glory - and rejecting the offer is on them. Meanwhile, taking off the board most of them allows me a fine escape, and if only one or two are left challenging me, \u0130 might even take them down before \u0130 leave, and further make it clear that it's better to stay away from me, no matter how much \u0130 stink of Wyrm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-01T08:21:05-07:00","id":1524869,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524796#post1524796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Cool. So what would you rule with 7 successes? \u0130t's still an intimidation roll, it still needs to have consequence, it still says the loser of the resisted roll by 1 success is cowed and these are 7.</div> </div> </div> </div>Allow me to reduce this to a level that may seem simpler to comprehend what we're telling you. Just because you have +10 to Persuasion and rolled a natural 20 to seduce the lovely barmaid means jack shit if she loves her husband and her children enough not to want to go to bed with you. She'll be kind to you and go out of the way to help you, but she is not going to ruin her life or her core relationships for you.<br /> <br /> This is the same thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-02T07:28:23-07:00","id":1524915,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524869#post1524869\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Allow me to reduce this to a level that may seem simpler to comprehend what we're telling you. Just because you have +10 to Persuasion and rolled a natural 20 to seduce the lovely barmaid means jack shit if she loves her husband and her children enough not to want to go to bed with you. She'll be kind to you and go out of the way to help you, but she is not going to ruin her life or her core relationships for you.<br /> <br /> This is the same thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think we actually think differently on how the game works. The game has plenty of traits and modifiers to represent people's feelings, convictions and attachments. They're a core element of the game. A barmaid in love with her husband and children may, by ST decision, get bonuses on her rolls to resist seduction. The intensity of the bonuses depends on what the ST feels is appropriate. She has her basic Wits + Selfcontrol (if being seduced is against her long term interests) or Conscience + Selfcontrol (if it is against her values) or whatever other combination of traits the ST feels is suitable to the interaction, plus a bonus for true love or whatever circumstantial bonus is at stake. The seducer has his/her rolls and whatever bonus/malus applies. In VDA20 seduction is done in 3 steps/rolls, so that's what I would use as the basis. If, after her being given quantified advantages based on her feelings and situation, the seducer still wins the rolls, that means to me, if I'm narrating, that he managed to seduce her, even if she's going to regret it later. We're humans and we have many strings that can be pulled, even when our hearts are genuinely into something. The spirit may be strong and the will clear, but maybe the flesh is weak and it just happened that she wasn't getting as much as she needed from her husband when the seducer passed by (low Self-control and maybe low temporary Willpower at the time), or maybe she loves her husband and children but is not too bright (low Wits) and the very attractive seducer was very skilled at confusing her and making her think that nobody would find out and that she would rarely get a chance at a night with such an attractive and charismatic guy, and it won't affect her in the long term anyway since none would know but the two of them. Or any other explanation, which I would prefer to craft on the moment based on how the different rolls go, so I can represent adequately the traits that rolled very well and those that rolled very poorly. I don't see any reason to give the barmaid a straight up immunity to seduction in a game with traits representing all the relevant factors.<br /> <br /> While I have abandoned the idea of the werewolves never converting due to their attachment and real knowledge of Gaia, I would also point out that they have a trait to represent this connection: Gnosis. Even on their own, they're susceptible to the Wyrm if their Rage score is above their Gnosis score and if they roll too many successes for frenzy. Vampires have Convictions and Road ratings, too. When a character has a bonus explicitly tied to rolls to convert people, I would not declare the attempt impossible. I would factor in the victim's traits that quantify their conviction - since they exist already in the character sheet -, give each person the bonuses and maluses and difficulty values and required number of successes that I think fit the situation, and I would let the game and the rolls decide how the Narrative goes. This gives more chances for drama and makes everyone feel the dots on their sheet are not just numbers, but actually represent something that will have dramatic effect and that quantify something true about the characters, including what is possible or impossible for them. I find no narrative value in giving essentially plot armor to characters who don't need it (and already have quantified game armor in such rolls) just because they love their husband or Gaia so much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-02T08:14:20-07:00","id":1524918,"title":"","content":"If you wanted to make an argument that you could slowly erode people's beliefs and lead them to break down on their core values, I'd be with you. You're not doing that with a single action and a single roll with a SKILL.<br /> <br /> Gnosis has no relation to how susceptible or not you are to the Wyrm. Wyrm taint comes from being exposed to corruption or doing morally bankrupt deeds.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-02T09:24:58-07:00","id":1524926,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524918#post1524918\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you wanted to make an argument that you could slowly erode people's beliefs and lead them to break down on their core values, I'd be with you. You're not doing that with a single action and a single roll with a SKILL.<br /> <br /> Gnosis has no relation to how susceptible or not you are to the Wyrm. Wyrm taint comes from being exposed to corruption or doing morally bankrupt deeds.</div> </div> </div> </div>First off, \u0130've already clarified in multiple posts that \u0130'm not trying to argue that that single roll of intimidation with those successes would do that. \u0130 opened the thread because \u0130 don't know what would happen with that many successes.<br /> <br /> Second, \u0130 think there are two main scenarios (relevant to the game, at least) for people to change their beliefs: 1) sudden overwhelming experience contradicting what one previously believed, followed by a better explanation of the world that accomodates the new experience, followed by smaller experiences that support the new explanation and belie the old belief; 2) a gradual and long manipulation which does the above but leaves the strong experience last as a sort of final blow.<br /> <br /> \u0130 would be comfortable ruling that if a character trying to convert others can cause an experience of the first kind and roll successes exceeding the victim's Conviction or Gnosis (Gnosis isn't how susceptible you are to the Wyrm: it's how deep you go in your experience of Gaia and the spirit world, and that ties in with your core beliefs. Even the name &quot;gnosis&quot; in real world philosophy and religion is sometimes used in relation to one's not only believed, but experienced reality, and yet \u0130 know that even in the real world that type of belief and experience can change or be re-interpreted, so Garou are not immune to paradigm shifts, though Gnosis goes from 1 to 10 and Conviction from 1 to 5, so they're definitely more resistant than vampires), they'd have succeeded in giving that type of first shock and may now move into more gradual indoctrination and sugarcoating to let the new idea take roots and grow. Left alone, the shocking experience will eventually be re-rationalised according to already familiar beliefs.<br /> Seeing a monster so scary because he can outdo the strongest Garous in an intimidation roll way more than most of their antagonists can, thanks to Road aura, etc., \u0130 think qualifies for shocking, and if the successes exceed one's Gnosis \u0130 would allow for it to give the individual a moment of pause. Garou suffer from Harano, for example. Such an experience could trigger a similar in kind, but not long term, response. &quot;Why does the Wyrm have such a servant, and none of us can seem to match him in strength? Sure, as a pack we can probably take him down quickly, but who knows what else he can do? \u0130 have faced Nexus Crawlers and this Set-worshipper hasn't shown a tiny bit of a NC's abilities, and yet he somehow manages to give me such a feeling of dread that \u0130 want to stop fighting and not risk my life and that of the tribe's young. Why? How could Set have become so powerful? What is happening, Gaia?&quot; says the alpha to himself, after losing the roll heavily. \u0130f the Setite leaves the camp, it ends there. The Garou will counsel each other, perform their rites and commune with the spirits until they have rationalised what has happened as nothing but another trick of the Wyrm. \u0130f, however, the Setite can risk staying longer and deliver more ideas and experiences with similar convincing power - unlikely, both because of the risk of werewolves eventually attacking him anyway at the first wrongly calculated word, and because there's only so much a Setite has in his arsenal, which is not really tailored for Garou, nor does a typical Setite understand Garou beliefs and experiences much - he might find a way to convert the werewolves to some very well curated and tailored form of Setism (ex: Egyptian gods are forms of some of the Celestines, but Set noticed that the Wyrm had gone mad and foreign horrors were going to destroy the world, so he became a human avatar and then accepted the Embrace to have the particular power needed to fight these foes. So he does use Wyrm means and tactics, but is actually protecting Gaia and trying to restore sanity to the Wyrm).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-02T12:51:31-07:00","id":1524930,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524926#post1524926\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u0130 would be comfortable ruling that if a character trying to convert others can cause an experience of the first kind and roll successes exceeding the victim's Conviction or Gnosis (Gnosis isn't how susceptible you are to the Wyrm: it's how deep you go in your experience of Gaia and the spirit world, and that ties in with your core beliefs. Even the name &quot;gnosis&quot; in real world philosophy and religion is sometimes used in relation to one's not only believed, but experienced reality, and yet \u0130 know that even in the real world that type of belief and experience can change or be re-interpreted, so Garou are not immune to paradigm shifts, though Gnosis goes from 1 to 10 and Conviction from 1 to 5, so they're definitely more resistant than vampires), they'd have succeeded in giving that type of first shock and may now move into more gradual indoctrination and sugarcoating to let the new idea take roots and grow. Left alone, the shocking experience will eventually be re-rationalised according to already familiar beliefs.</div> </div> </div> </div>This really isn't how Gnosis works in WtA (I agree with some of what you're saying, but that's why I think Gnosis is a terrible name for the trait in question). WtA uses Willpower for anything that would be covered by Virtues in VtM.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Seeing a monster so scary because he can outdo the strongest Garous in an intimidation roll way more than most of their antagonists can, thanks to Road aura, etc.,... </div> </div> </div>This is a serious &quot;facts not in evidence,&quot; statement. I can make a starting level Garou that can throw 18 dice with a -1 difficulty to Strength + Intimidate other Garou (-3 if you go by the strict wording of a W20 core Merit even if the spirit of the Merit would indicate it wouldn't apply to this), that comes with an additional benefit of your successes having significantly greater impact than mundane rolls should normally accomplish (the Persuasion Gift is exceedingly broken even in the hands of groups not trying to break the game with it). And I can stack on considerably more if I wanted to make a Garou (esp. with pack support) that's an absolute badass at using physical intimidation to get their way via experience (and XP). There's a reason Rank 2+ Gifts that work in the Intimidation space don't generally bother with boosting mundane rolls and just move on into super-intimidation (like Staredown, which is just a Rank 2 Gift, which is a diff 5 roll against non-Garou, can with 5+ successes, cause your Setite to just stand still in fear until attacked and can't even spend WP to do anything about it).<br /> <br /> The fact that WtA generally opts for Gifts/etc with direct supernatural effect. rather than super-pumping up mundane rolls makes this more complicated to compare, but ultimately is just a stylistic choice that's enabled by WtA's Gifts not having a set progression like Disciplines, and along with Talens/Fetishes/Totems, so individual powers can just do whatever they're designed to do.<br /> <br /> The idea that the Garou never face this sort of stuff because WtA handles the numbers/powers interactions differently isn't really a great argument.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Why does the Wyrm have such a servant, and none of us can seem to match him in strength? Sure, as a pack we can probably take him down quickly, but who knows what else he can do? \u0130 have faced Nexus Crawlers and this Set-worshipper hasn't shown a tiny bit of a NC's abilities, and yet he somehow manages to give me such a feeling of dread that \u0130 want to stop fighting and not risk my life and that of the tribe's young. Why? How could Set have become so powerful? What is happening, Gaia?&quot; says the alpha to himself, after losing the roll heavily. </div> </div> </div>This doesn't make a lick of sense. Again, it feels like you've looked at how spirit traits cap at 10, and thus a Nexus Crawler can only throw 10 dice at things. But it's forgetting that spirit Charms are built with this in mind. A high level Nexus Crawler (who might have effectively 100 health levels in the Umbra) needs 5 successes, once, on a Warp Reality roll to wipe out a pack of Garou. And Nexus Cralwers aren't even <b>physical</b> powerhouses by bane standards (not that they're weak, it's just not their nature to worry about physical combat as a primary means of achieving their goals). WtA antagonists are highly varied and frequently have extremely niche specializations where &quot;weak&quot; Wyrm servants are still more dangerous in their specialization than many high level threats are (fomori are particularly prone to this; if a character has fought Nexus Cralwers, plural, they've dealt with some Enticers before too, who are ridiculously good at supernatural seduction for their &quot;weight class&quot; as foes).<br /> <br /> I get that you're narrating a massive number of successes in the Setite's favor, but that doesn't mean it's a reaction that makes any sense. The Garou aren't going to be flustered by the concept of a Wyrm creature that's ridiculously good at intimidation. The Wyrm is a hydra with many heads and many skills. The most powerful of the Thunderwyrms is so massive, and so strong, she can knock over large buildings by accidentally bumping into them.<br /> <br /> Also keep in mind that Garou, like Mages (even if weaker) have an innate ability to sense if supernatural powers are in play. Even if they can't do anything about Serpentis's social roll buffing, they can feel that they're being subjected to supernaturally boosted rolls and rationalize that later. Existentially dread and completely questioning of their faith isn't going to happen when they can just say, &quot;those damned leeches and their abilities to toy with emotions have caused me to act cowardly and foolishly instead of following my duty and slaughtering them.&quot;<br /> <br /> It's just completely out of step with WtA to consider a vampire that can pull this off some sort of mind shattering event for a Garou. They know vampires exist, and are dangerous.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u0130f, however, the Setite can risk staying longer and deliver more ideas and experiences with similar convincing power - unlikely, both because of the risk of werewolves eventually attacking him anyway at the first wrongly calculated word, and because there's only so much a Setite has in his arsenal, which is not really tailored for Garou, nor does a typical Setite understand Garou beliefs and experiences much - he might find a way to convert the werewolves to some very well curated and tailored form of Setism (ex: Egyptian gods are forms of some of the Celestines, but Set noticed that the Wyrm had gone mad and foreign horrors were going to destroy the world, so he became a human avatar and then accepted the Embrace to have the particular power needed to fight these foes. So he does use Wyrm means and tactics, but is actually protecting Gaia and trying to restore sanity to the Wyrm). </div> </div> </div>And this is when I, as the ST, would have a problem because it's clear you're trying for Manipulation based actions, not Strength ones. If your specific wording matters, it's Manipulation. No Strength buffing with blood and Potence helps you there.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-02T12:53:06-07:00","id":1524931,"title":"","content":"Having Rage higher than Gnosis only makes you a friendly fire danger when you frenzy. There is no susceptibility to taint from Rage, no matter what kind of picture Thrall of the Wyrm tries to paint. In fact, there is no trait that reflects Taint, though there are some suggestions in some books. In any case, there's a rite to get rid of it.<br /> <br /> Having any path other than Humanity generally makes you stink according to Sense Wyrm... assuming any Garou bothers to use it. If they know you're a vampire, that's usually a good enough reason to kill you on sight at this point in time, and a sept of huge amounts of Shadow Lords* is going to host some very, <b>very</b> Renown-hungry members. Again, sliding up and trying to scare them this way is basically ringing the dinner bell, and the successes just indicate your perceived value in Glory. It's literally the worst decision to try and do in the middle of territory that Garou will not retreat from and have dug into for generations. Some spirits also probably saw you, since your presence in the Middle Umbra is a blot that Obfuscate <i>cannot hide</i>.<br /> <br /> *Septs in the dark ages also tended to be very mono-tribal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-02T14:33:24-07:00","id":1524935,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524930#post1524930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This really isn't how Gnosis works in WtA (I agree with some of what you're saying, but that's why I think Gnosis is a terrible name for the trait in question). WtA uses Willpower for anything that would be covered by Virtues in VtM.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This is a serious &quot;facts not in evidence,&quot; statement. I can make a starting level Garou that can throw 18 dice with a -1 difficulty to Strength + Intimidate other Garou (-3 if you go by the strict wording of a W20 core Merit even if the spirit of the Merit would indicate it wouldn't apply to this), that comes with an additional benefit of your successes having significantly greater impact than mundane rolls should normally accomplish (the Persuasion Gift is exceedingly broken even in the hands of groups not trying to break the game with it). And I can stack on considerably more if I wanted to make a Garou (esp. with pack support) that's an absolute badass at using physical intimidation to get their way via experience (and XP). There's a reason Rank 2+ Gifts that work in the Intimidation space don't generally bother with boosting mundane rolls and just move on into super-intimidation (like Staredown, which is just a Rank 2 Gift, which is a diff 5 roll against non-Garou, can with 5+ successes, cause your Setite to just stand still in fear until attacked and can't even spend WP to do anything about it).<br /> <br /> The fact that WtA generally opts for Gifts/etc with direct supernatural effect. rather than super-pumping up mundane rolls makes this more complicated to compare, but ultimately is just a stylistic choice that's enabled by WtA's Gifts not having a set progression like Disciplines, and along with Talens/Fetishes/Totems, so individual powers can just do whatever they're designed to do.<br /> <br /> The idea that the Garou never face this sort of stuff because WtA handles the numbers/powers interactions differently isn't really a great argument.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This doesn't make a lick of sense. Again, it feels like you've looked at how spirit traits cap at 10, and thus a Nexus Crawler can only throw 10 dice at things. But it's forgetting that spirit Charms are built with this in mind. A high level Nexus Crawler (who might have effectively 100 health levels in the Umbra) needs 5 successes, once, on a Warp Reality roll to wipe out a pack of Garou. And Nexus Cralwers aren't even <b>physical</b> powerhouses by bane standards (not that they're weak, it's just not their nature to worry about physical combat as a primary means of achieving their goals). WtA antagonists are highly varied and frequently have extremely niche specializations where &quot;weak&quot; Wyrm servants are still more dangerous in their specialization than many high level threats are (fomori are particularly prone to this; if a character has fought Nexus Cralwers, plural, they've dealt with some Enticers before too, who are ridiculously good at supernatural seduction for their &quot;weight class&quot; as foes).<br /> <br /> I get that you're narrating a massive number of successes in the Setite's favor, but that doesn't mean it's a reaction that makes any sense. The Garou aren't going to be flustered by the concept of a Wyrm creature that's ridiculously good at intimidation. The Wyrm is a hydra with many heads and many skills. The most powerful of the Thunderwyrms is so massive, and so strong, she can knock over large buildings by accidentally bumping into them.<br /> <br /> Also keep in mind that Garou, like Mages (even if weaker) have an innate ability to sense if supernatural powers are in play. Even if they can't do anything about Serpentis's social roll buffing, they can feel that they're being subjected to supernaturally boosted rolls and rationalize that later. Existentially dread and completely questioning of their faith isn't going to happen when they can just say, &quot;those damned leeches and their abilities to toy with emotions have caused me to act cowardly and foolishly instead of following my duty and slaughtering them.&quot;<br /> <br /> It's just completely out of step with WtA to consider a vampire that can pull this off some sort of mind shattering event for a Garou. They know vampires exist, and are dangerous.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> And this is when I, as the ST, would have a problem because it's clear you're trying for Manipulation based actions, not Strength ones. If your specific wording matters, it's Manipulation. No Strength buffing with blood and Potence helps you there.</div> </div> </div> </div>What \u0130 suggested, and it was an example using what we already discussed, not an attempt to revalidate the already much aborted and banished idea of converting Garou through the single \u0130ntimidation roll, is that if any character, using any means, can cause a shocking experience with some religious overtone to it, one could, as an option to narrate characters for whom converting others is an important thing, consider Conviction or Gnosis as the success benchmark for when a shock may occur. \u0130 also specifically said that the shock is not sufficient on its own, but needs to be followed up quickly by more work, or else the experience will be rationalised according to previous beliefs. The indoctrination work done after in that scenario is not the initial Strength + \u0130ntimidation roll done which should have caused a temporary shock. And yes, the later work may include Persuasion and also Charisma rolls, but this is all in what \u0130 said above.<br /> <br /> Some Virtue rolls are replaced (in a turned around way) by Rage. \u0130 recall reading the Gnosis is the connection and understanding the Garou has with the spirit world. Garou don't &quot;believe&quot;, except in terms of paradigm. They don't need faith in rituals because they see them working regularly and know the spirits that make them work on the other side.<br /> That's also why, \u0130 would think it impossible to convert a Garou to Setism based on some lies about religions, and a more curated approach is required, to not challenge what Garou undeniably know by experience, whilst also giving them a reason to think Set is cool and his methods are secretly cool. Hence, \u0130 have long left the idea of conversion of Garous in a single roll.<br /> <br /> At this point \u0130 need to ask people to realise that \u0130 have long grasped, since the conversation started, that a single roll with 1000 successes won't instantly convert Garou and the focus of the convo has shifted elsewhere.<br /> <br /> As for your comments about the mechanics between WtA and VtM, that may be a fair point: they may have been written so differently that the comparison does not work too well. But \u0130 still can't find it in myself to make a whole splat immune to a whole social interaction without this immunity being spelt out in their manual. And maybe the words of the alpha would not be so dramatic and include mention of Nexus Crawlers, but they would be an expression of having been outdone by 7 successes. <br /> Of course, if you can make starting characters who roll 18 dice at -1 difficulty on a Strength + \u0130ntimidation roll, then that's completely fine and it changes the interaction entirely because the vampire simply won't even win the role, or will win it by a much smaller margin. \u0130 don't know how to do that in WtA because I'm not that familiar with it. All \u0130 noticed and remember now was that they get to Strength 9 in Crinos and can have up to \u0130ntimidate 5, get 2 more dice if their totem is Grandfather Thunder and double their Strength with Might of Thor (but not everyone has Might of Thor). But still, it does not make sense to me to.give immunity to all because some can win the contrasted roll or face regularly scarier stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-02T14:42:41-07:00","id":1524936,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524931#post1524931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having Rage higher than Gnosis only makes you a friendly fire danger when you frenzy. There is no susceptibility to taint from Rage, no matter what kind of picture Thrall of the Wyrm tries to paint. In fact, there is no trait that reflects Taint, though there are some suggestions in some books. In any case, there's a rite to get rid of it.<br /> <br /> Having any path other than Humanity generally makes you stink according to Sense Wyrm... assuming any Garou bothers to use it. If they know you're a vampire, that's usually a good enough reason to kill you on sight at this point in time, and a sept of huge amounts of Shadow Lords* is going to host some very, <b>very</b> Renown-hungry members. Again, sliding up and trying to scare them this way is basically ringing the dinner bell, and the successes just indicate your perceived value in Glory. It's literally the worst decision to try and do in the middle of territory that Garou will not retreat from and have dug into for generations. Some spirits also probably saw you, since your presence in the Middle Umbra is a blot that Obfuscate <i>cannot hide</i>.<br /> <br /> *Septs in the dark ages also tended to be very mono-tribal.</div> </div> </div> </div>As \u0130 said before, \u0130'm way past that specific roll. \u0130 actually did it in game, but they had some fetish that raised my difficulty further and then the ST rolled a ton of 10s, so it didn't work except against few and we forfeited the next session because characters were surely dead.<br /> As for the spirits spying on me from before, yes, and they did track me for a bit. To be fair, when \u0130 went there, neither i nor my character knew spirits did this. Most \u0130've learned about WtA has been in the past few weeks, after deciding to go in their territory obfuscated. So my character learned not all Garou die in one turn on castle walls, and \u0130 learned many more things about them. Not my favourite splat, to be honest, but alas.<br /> \u0130 think if \u0130 storytell in the future, \u0130m going to avoid crossovers unless \u0130 play new WoD or make up rules for other splats as antagonists from the book of the splat we're playing (like Garou being statted as if having equivalents to Disciplines). Makes my life easier and prevents debates on how to compare rolls and traits.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-02T22:25:10-07:00","id":1524960,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524935#post1524935\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for your comments about the mechanics between WtA and VtM, that may be a fair point: they may have been written so differently that the comparison does not work too well. But \u0130 still can't find it in myself to make a whole splat immune to a whole social interaction without this immunity being spelt out in their manual. And maybe the words of the alpha would not be so dramatic and include mention of Nexus Crawlers, but they would be an expression of having been outdone by 7 successes.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not the leader (by any name used) that would rouse everyone; that's the Galliard's job as the one who remembers everything that The People have been through and is ready to drop the story down on them at a moment's notice. Assuming you're not playing W5, anyway. Eyy-oooo!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course, if you can make starting characters who roll 18 dice at -1 difficulty on a Strength + \u0130ntimidation roll, then that's completely fine and it changes the interaction entirely because the vampire simply won't even win the role, or will win it by a much smaller margin. \u0130 don't know how to do that in WtA because I'm not that familiar with it. All \u0130 noticed and remember now was that they get to Strength 9 in Crinos and can have up to \u0130ntimidate 5, get 2 more dice if their totem is Grandfather Thunder and double their Strength with Might of Thor (but not everyone has Might of Thor). But still, it does not make sense to me to.give immunity to all because some can win the contrasted roll or face regularly scarier stuff. </div> </div> </div>Might of Thor would be somewhat uncommon for Shadow Lords. What they do have is a curse that causes ranged attacks to veer toward you, a Gift that's very much like the effect of Presence 5 (very specifically that one five-dot effect, not, like, all of Presence up to 5 dots), as well as the ability to cut you by attacking your shadow. Also, if someone frenzies and happens to have Rage higher than Gnosis, they can keep them from perpetrating friendly fire incidents. They're also one of the tribes to get the silver soak boost, and their capacity to discern where anything supernatural is within a rather large personal envelope (not a region, but anything even remotely nearby), as well as if it's aligned to the Wyrm or not.<br /> <br /> But, those are all unfamiliar territory, and if it's not something that you want to deal with, no problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-02T23:54:57-07:00","id":1524961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524935#post1524935\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What \u0130 suggested, and it was an example using what we already discussed, not an attempt to revalidate the already much aborted and banished idea of converting Garou through the single \u0130ntimidation roll, is that if any character, using any means, can cause a shocking experience with some religious overtone to it,</div> </div> </div> </div>I was responding to this assertion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> consider Conviction or Gnosis as the success benchmark for when a shock may occur. </div> </div> </div>For someone that's insisting on things from the manual, you're being very resistant to the idea that Gnosis doesn't do this for werewolves, Willpower does (and in fact does for most splats in the WoD since most don't use VtM style Virtues).<br /> <br /> Gnosis is, essentially, a Garou's spiritual potency. It's how much spiritual energy they can channel, and whole well they can assert themselves within the spirit hierarchies of the Umbra and its denizens. That's why Gnosis does things like roll to move between worlds, activate Talens/Fetishes, and so on (and really not a lot of so on besides specific powers). All of the &quot;being in tune with Gaia&quot; aspects of it are how they regain temporary Gnosis, and raise permanent Gnosis.<br /> <br /> It makes no sense to have Gnosis be this benchmark, and WtA uses Willpower for this in similar situations.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u0130 also specifically said that the shock is not sufficient on its own, but needs to be followed up quickly by more work, or else the experience will be rationalised according to previous beliefs. The indoctrination work done after in that scenario is not the initial Strength + \u0130ntimidation roll done which should have caused a temporary shock. And yes, the later work may include Persuasion and also Charisma rolls, but this is all in what \u0130 said above. </div> </div> </div>What I'm objecting to is what your posting qualifying as this: &quot;sudden overwhelming experience contradicting what one previously believed.&quot;<br /> <br /> You haven't actually posted anything that would shock a Garou to this level, making any follow up rolls pointless. What you're talking about is, as others have also noted, affirming Garou beliefs about vampires and their connection to the Wyrm. They might be stunned temporarily by the terror of how potent said vampire is (though they're get a reflexive roll to sense that it might be more terrifying than it should be for what it is), but there's nothing presented that's going to make them question what they believe. The Wyrm corrupting human forms into ridiculously powerful monsters is a core tenet of their cultural belief that they were shaped by Gaia to be her warriors to fight and slay such things.<br /> <br /> What your describing is more like a hunter moving to collect a deer, only to run into a grizzly bear that wants that kill for itself. A grizzly bear is a pants-shittingly horrifying thing to accidentally run into, even with a high powered hunting rifle on you, but hunters know bears exist and bears will try to take kills from humans, so it's not some radical shock to core and fundamental beliefs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Some Virtue rolls are replaced (in a turned around way) by Rage. </div> </div> </div>The only place this is really the case is how the two games handle frenzies. Vampires roll to resist frenzy with their Virtues (or guide them for Instinct); with the implication being that once provoked, a frenzy will happen unless overcome. Garou roll to Frenzy, so provocations are not presumed to automatically turn into them without resistance; if a Garou wants to resist Frenzy rather they use Willpower for that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Garou don't &quot;believe&quot;, except in terms of paradigm. They don't need faith in rituals because they see them working regularly and know the spirits that make them work on the other side </div> </div> </div>Garou do believe as well. One of the truly scariest things about the Garou is that they have faith and facts that reinforce them. They have not only a religion with dogma taken on faith, but also divine beings affirming that they are right to do so. The Garou Nation is a culture of religious fanaticism; even more so in the past. The Black Spirals might put their faith in the Wyrm, but they're just as much a mix of faith and fact as well. In modern nights, there are more dissidents that don't fully believe, but that's still a relative rarity precisely because the few points where the Garou are clearly taking things on faith instead of fact (despite Gaia, Luna, and the Incarnae around) are rather academic compared to more immediate concerns.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But \u0130 still can't find it in myself to make a whole splat immune to a whole social interaction without this immunity being spelt out in their manual. </div> </div> </div>Garou aren't immune to intimidation. Intimidation is one of the three traditional forms of Garou dominance challenges, and is frequently used to exert authority and hierarchy. Garou are not immune to demoralization, long term psychological tactics, and so on.<br /> <br /> What we're saying is that you're using the wrong approach, so it isn't going to have the desired effect. The &quot;immunity,&quot; isn't in the rules, because they're not broadly immune. You're just opting for one of the worst possible approaches to all of this.<br /> <br /> It's like the Cuban Missile Crisis scene in X-Men: First Class, where Charles is trying to talk Erik out of tossing all the live munitions the US and Soviet warships just fired right back at them and killing all the sailors. Charlies picks pretty much the stupidest phrase possible to a very pissed off Holocaust survivor. &quot;They're just following orders.&quot; It's not that Erik is immune to Charles's persuasive abilities (in fact the movie shows a few times that even without his powers Charles can convince Erik of things just fine), but in that case the persuasion completely backfired by being &quot;successful.&quot; Erik wants to kill military people that are following orders to murder instead of rejecting those orders as illegal and immoral. Convincing him that he's facing such people is just increasing his motivation to use lethal force.<br /> <br /> The Garou aren't immune to intimidation, you're just coming up with things that would intimidate most Garou to do the exact opposite of what you're stated goals are.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u0130 don't know how to do that in WtA because I'm not that familiar with it. </div> </div> </div>So, it doesn't feel like this acknowledgement of a lack of familiarity is translating into really listening to what those of us more familiar with it are saying. We can be wrong about things despite being more familiar of course. We're not perfect or speaking gospel truths, but there's a point where it starts to feel like you don't really want the perspectives of people more familiar with WtA than yourself because you're getting a lot of answers that don't seem like you find them satisfying.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-03T00:01:02-07:00","id":1524962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524960#post1524960\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's not the leader (by any name used) that would rouse everyone; that's the Galliard's job as the one who remembers everything that The People have been through and is ready to drop the story down on them at a moment's notice. Assuming you're not playing W5, anyway. Eyy-oooo!<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Might of Thor would be somewhat uncommon for Shadow Lords. What they do have is a curse that causes ranged attacks to veer toward you, a Gift that's very much like the effect of Presence 5 (very specifically that one five-dot effect, not, like, all of Presence up to 5 dots), as well as the ability to cut you by attacking your shadow. Also, if someone frenzies and happens to have Rage higher than Gnosis, they can keep them from perpetrating friendly fire incidents. They're also one of the tribes to get the silver soak boost, and their capacity to discern where anything supernatural is within a rather large personal envelope (not a region, but anything even remotely nearby), as well as if it's aligned to the Wyrm or not.<br /> <br /> But, those are all unfamiliar territory, and if it's not something that you want to deal with, no problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, \u0130 mentioned Might of Thor in relation to the more general question of how many bonuses do werewolves (not just Shadow Lords) have on Strength + \u0130ntimidation rolls. The Shadow Lords gifts \u0130 had seen, and some are cool, but \u0130 can't recall any that modifies \u0130ntimidation rolls as such, so \u0130 didn't mention them. But that's also why \u0130 am sceptic about giving the Shadow Lords a bonus on intimidation rolls just because they hang out with fear spirits by lore... That's kind of fluff stuff which \u0130 would give mechanical weight to only if it was stated explicitly. Or if someone homebrewed a gift or rite or fetish of that kind, which would make total sense if they hang out with such spirits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-03T03:15:33-07:00","id":1524964,"title":"","content":"Who said, &quot;The Shadow Lords should get a bonus to Intimidation just because of their spirit allies with no additional steps?&quot;<br /> <br /> Bringing up the spirits Shadow Lords are familiar with is precisely because of things like those are spirits they have an easier time make Talens.<br /> <br /> Also, Shadow Lords don't have a lot of direct Intimidation boosts because (1) again, WtA Gifts don't do a lot pure boosts to mundane rolls, and (2) they're not werewolves that default to &quot;Rawr! I'm big and scary! Do what I say or suffer!&quot; The Shadow Lords get a ton of information gathering Gifts so they can do things like force confessions out of people, or blackmail them, as well as some super-intimidation Gifts (like the aforementioned &quot;I'm so scary you can't attack me without spending WP and rolling WP that has to beat my activation roll). The Shadow Lords have a reputation of being sneaky, conniving, and backstabbers for a reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-03T11:25:36-07:00","id":1524980,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524964#post1524964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Who said, &quot;The Shadow Lords should get a bonus to Intimidation just because of their spirit allies with no additional steps?&quot;<br /> <br /> Bringing up the spirits Shadow Lords are familiar with is precisely because of things like those are spirits they have an easier time make Talens.<br /> <br /> Also, Shadow Lords don't have a lot of direct Intimidation boosts because (1) again, WtA Gifts don't do a lot pure boosts to mundane rolls, and (2) they're not werewolves that default to &quot;Rawr! I'm big and scary! Do what I say or suffer!&quot; The Shadow Lords get a ton of information gathering Gifts so they can do things like force confessions out of people, or blackmail them, as well as some super-intimidation Gifts (like the aforementioned &quot;I'm so scary you can't attack me without spending WP and rolling WP that has to beat my activation roll). The Shadow Lords have a reputation of being sneaky, conniving, and backstabbers for a reason.</div> </div> </div> </div>it was another commenter, can't remember who, who had suggested they were extra immune/resistant due to dealing with fear spirits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-03T15:42:45-07:00","id":1524996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524980#post1524980\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> it was another commenter, can't remember who, who had suggested they were extra immune/resistant due to dealing with fear spirits.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23890_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23890\" data-vbnamecard=\"23890\">Shadeprowler</a> said. He said that they are used to dealing with Fear spirits as many of those teach them the gifts they use.<br /> You seem to be hell bent on codifying every narrative element into mechanics. It's not going to get you far in the WoD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-04T12:20:34-07:00","id":1525028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1524996#post1524996\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's not what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23890_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23890\" data-vbnamecard=\"23890\">Shadeprowler</a> said. He said that they are used to dealing with Fear spirits as many of those teach them the gifts they use.<br /> You seem to be hell bent on codifying every narrative element into mechanics. It's not going to get you far in the WoD.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what \u0130 understood he meant since we were talking about why the intimidation wouldn't work. And if you deny a roll that normally has a stated outcome (the VDA20 manual says those who lose the contrasted roll in \u0130ntimidation are &quot;effectively cowed&quot;), you are creating a mechanic anyway. You are making someone immune to something. That may be reasonable, depending on each case, but it's still a mechanic of a sort.<br /> <br /> \u0130 have been following the WoD for almost 20 years, and \u0130 don't find it difficult. Some people might like more mechanics, some less. At the end of the day, there's still a need for final decisions in game to make sense to ST and players so that harmony is preserved and things don't seem arbitrary or get misunderstood. \u0130f at your table it works to make decisions as you're proposing, nothing wrong with it. Personally, \u0130 find the ST System to be the one where it is easiest to mechanize narrative, given how many traits are there to represent mental and moral states and faculties, and \u0130 think it's very fitting since this helps consistently portray the drama of not being in full control of oneself, which is quite significant to several splats.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-04T13:54:45-07:00","id":1525029,"title":"","content":"Well, you say the WoD, but it seems like more games don't use Strength + Intimidation as an off the shelf option (Werewolf, Mage, Changeling) than do (Vampire and Wraith). Also, technically, the procedure for breaking shit is a Feat of Strength - a Willpower roll (difficulty 9) to push yourself unless you perform some feat less than or equal to your Strength (with an unhelpfully small number of examples!). Being practiced in threatening others doesn't inherently make you more likely to break things, so you can probably see where this roll can kind of break down in play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-04T15:46:49-07:00","id":1525038,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1525029#post1525029\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, you say the WoD, but it seems like more games don't use Strength + Intimidation as an off the shelf option (Werewolf, Mage, Changeling) than do (Vampire and Wraith). Also, technically, the procedure for breaking shit is a Feat of Strength - a Willpower roll (difficulty 9) to push yourself unless you perform some feat less than or equal to your Strength (with an unhelpfully small number of examples!). Being practiced in threatening others doesn't inherently make you more likely to break things, so you can probably see where this roll can kind of break down in play.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not following why breaking stuff is being brought up. I just slammed my tail as part of the intimidation. Body language + whatever I was saying (e.g. &quot;surrender to Set or die&quot;). Though the point of involving the tail off-game was to justify using the higher Strength rating of the tail over that of the rest of the body (the tail's rating is 3 dots higher). But it was an extra anyway: as far as my understanding of intimidation rolls is concerned, direct threats of physical violence are handled through a Strength + Intimidation roll whether you make a display of strength or not. I think the intention of the writers was to use the Strength rating to represent how physically menacing one appears based on muscle size and the like. It is a questionable system, since some forms of Strength (like Potence) do not necessarily appear as big muscles and some factors are ignored (ex: what if you're threatening someone by holding a gun, or even a sword? Would it not make more sense to roll the damage rating of the weapon you're showing to the opponent, rather than Strength?), but I don't think that one has to always put up a show of strength in order to do the roll. I am open to refining the system to include more factors, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-04T18:21:44-07:00","id":1525049,"title":"","content":"How do the intimidatees know enough to be afraid, and how is it expressed by the would-be intimidator? If you don't break shit, casually leave an impression of your foot or tail in stone, or otherwise visibly demonstrate your supposed might, there isn't really a threat of anything. That's part of a reason why Strength doesn't work in this pairing, but performing a feat with Strength and then making a Manipulation + Intimidation roll with a bonus does. Body language, too, isn't in Strength; that's more Charisma or Appearance's territory, and why Garou have Charisma + Intimidation contests to see who blinks first.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-05T02:37:40-08:00","id":1525059,"title":"","content":"There's nothing wrong with the concept of a Strength based Intimidation roll All the WoD games are meant for the majority of the &quot;dramatic systems&quot; to be examples of how to use the rules to their full extent, not lock of creative uses of unusual combinations.<br /> <br /> That said, the Attribute still has to match the essential intent of the action. WtA has plenty of examples of places where different Attributes can be rolled for different intents.<br /> <br /> I think the issue remains wanting a Manipulation result out of a Strength roll because of all the Strength bonuses. Body language + whatever I was saying only works if what you're saying is in-line internally. Slamming your tail with such force is great for, &quot;any fool that seeks to fight me will die.&quot; The body language and words are in-line with each other. Slamming your tail down with incredible force and saying, &quot;accept in your hearts that mutton in superior in all ways to pork or die!&quot; doesn't work no matter how strong you are. People might say mutton is great to avoid the fight, but you're not really challenging their believes because raw strength doesn't challenge such beliefs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-05T05:59:54-08:00","id":1525067,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1525059#post1525059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's nothing wrong with the concept of a Strength based Intimidation roll All the WoD games are meant for the majority of the &quot;dramatic systems&quot; to be examples of how to use the rules to their full extent, not lock of creative uses of unusual combinations.<br /> <br /> That said, the Attribute still has to match the essential intent of the action. WtA has plenty of examples of places where different Attributes can be rolled for different intents.<br /> <br /> I think the issue remains wanting a Manipulation result out of a Strength roll because of all the Strength bonuses. Body language + whatever I was saying only works if what you're saying is in-line internally. Slamming your tail with such force is great for, &quot;any fool that seeks to fight me will die.&quot; The body language and words are in-line with each other. Slamming your tail down with incredible force and saying, &quot;accept in your hearts that mutton in superior in all ways to pork or die!&quot; doesn't work no matter how strong you are. People might say mutton is great to avoid the fight, but you're not really challenging their believes because raw strength doesn't challenge such beliefs.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u0130 agree. \u0130n fact the second scenario you suggested was never in the equation. From the first post, the sought result was a surrender, phrased in common language of the time &quot;convert or die&quot;, which does not mean &quot;accept in your heart or die&quot; in the context of religious encounters of the Middle Ages, since fake conversions were logically expected if forced. Even the thread title said &quot;(superficially) converting to Setism&quot;. The only reason why true conversion was raised as an issue was because of the apparent statistical likelihood of an overwhelming rate of success, which goes out of the window anyway if one starts factoring in other bonuses they might have to resist intimidation. So \u0130 don't think it needed to be a Manipulation roll.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-05T15:30:18-08:00","id":1525091,"title":"","content":"That feels... very ahistoric. The time-frame of the Dark Ages games did not include a lot of wars with forced conversions. It's been a long time since the violent Christian hegemony succeeded in securing most of Europe, and only the Moorish expanding into Iberia (and the Moors didn't force conversions as a common practice, and the Christian counter offensive that worked hasn't happened yet), and Ottoman wars in the east were the only major religious conflicts in the setting.<br /> <br /> The real world Inquisitions were very focused on rooting out fake conversions (the large number of them after the success of the Reconquista was the origin of the Spanish Inquisition).<br /> <br /> The idea that &quot;surrender or die,&quot; and &quot;convert or die&quot; would be seen as interchangeable doesn't really mesh with normal life in the time period the game is set in. <br /> <br /> As to the game itself, Strength + Intimidation still doesn't make sense for forced false conversions. Religions don't have five second conversions, even for forced ones. Forcing a fake conversion to a religion your target has never heard of makes even less sense. Just like &quot;surrender to King Bob&quot; doesn't work if nobody you're talking to has ever heard of any Kingdom ruled by Bob.<br /> <br /> If your intent needs to present these sorts of contexts to make sense, you're moving into Manipulation territory, not Strength.<br /> <br /> And more so, I would not allow an Aura bonus if you're not actually trying to convert. The bonus isn't for getting people to say &quot;hail Set&quot; if you know they don't mean it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-05T16:56:50-08:00","id":1525097,"title":"","content":"I think it would be very difficult to intimidate werewolves into doing anything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-05T18:27:48-08:00","id":1525102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1525097#post1525097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it would be very difficult to intimidate werewolves into doing anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seems like something that a permanent Renown roll could blunt. It's not just there to track Rank eligibility.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-05T20:26:32-08:00","id":1525105,"title":"","content":"Garou are religious fanatics who routinely interact with aspects of their religion, routinely fight horrible monsters, and are filled with supernatural rage. It\u2019s hard to imagine a thinking being that would be harder to intimidate into conversion.<br /> <br /> I would rule that such intimidation would be impossible. You might be able to make them run away or even say something that they don\u2019t believe, but actually changing their world views to such an extreme degree would be impossible without some sort of powerful use of Dominate or Presence. <br /> <br /> It does make sense within some contexts for Strength to be used for intimidation. If a strongman (Str 5) threatens to beat up a skinny nerd (Str 1) it\u2019s going to be intimidating regardless of that strongman\u2019s social skills. Then again, the difference in combat prowess between the strongman and nerd is apparent. If the nerd was carrying a gun, suddenly that Strength score doesn\u2019t mean as much. All werewolves carry guns in this scenario from their various supernatural powers. Also, unlike the strong man, a super strong vampire isn\u2019t obviously strong unless it displays that strength in some way. An Antediluvian with 10 Strength and 10 Potence may be stronger than a giant Wyrm beast, but that doesn\u2019t really come across unless it displays that strength spectacularly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-06T01:14:37-08:00","id":1525109,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1524237-intimidating-werewolves-into-superficially-converting-to-setism?p=1525091#post1525091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That feels... very ahistoric. The time-frame of the Dark Ages games did not include a lot of wars with forced conversions. It's been a long time since the violent Christian hegemony succeeded in securing most of Europe, and only the Moorish expanding into Iberia (and the Moors didn't force conversions as a common practice, and the Christian counter offensive that worked hasn't happened yet), and Ottoman wars in the east were the only major religious conflicts in the setting.<br /> <br /> The real world Inquisitions were very focused on rooting out fake conversions (the large number of them after the success of the Reconquista was the origin of the Spanish Inquisition).<br /> <br /> The idea that &quot;surrender or die,&quot; and &quot;convert or die&quot; would be seen as interchangeable doesn't really mesh with normal life in the time period the game is set in.<br /> <br /> As to the game itself, Strength + Intimidation still doesn't make sense for forced false conversions. Religions don't have five second conversions, even for forced ones. Forcing a fake conversion to a religion your target has never heard of makes even less sense. Just like &quot;surrender to King Bob&quot; doesn't work if nobody you're talking to has ever heard of any Kingdom ruled by Bob.<br /> <br /> If your intent needs to present these sorts of contexts to make sense, you're moving into Manipulation territory, not Strength.<br /> <br /> And more so, I would not allow an Aura bonus if you're not actually trying to convert. The bonus isn't for getting people to say &quot;hail Set&quot; if you know they don't mean it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, those are fair points. Nothing to retort.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}